== Introduction ==

=== Abstract ===

This document specifies trusted-dealer key generation for the FROST threshold signing protocol specified in [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445], specializing the trusted-dealer construction described in [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591#name-trusted-dealer-key-generati RFC 9591 Appendix C, "Trusted Dealer Key Generation"].

A dealer constructs a ''t-of-n'' Shamir secret-sharing scheme for a nonzero secp256k1 threshold secret using a polynomial of degree at most ''t - 1'' whose constant term is the threshold secret. The dealer publishes Feldman Verifiable Secret Sharing (VSS) commitments to the polynomial coefficients and securely distributes a corresponding secret share to each participant. Participant identifiers are the integers ''0, ..., n-1''. The Shamir evaluation point for participant ''id'' is the corresponding nonzero scalar ''id + 1''. The secret polynomial is evaluated at each participant's corresponding Shamir evaluation point to produce the participant secret shares. Each participant independently verifies its secret share against the VSS commitments and derives its public share from the corresponding polynomial evaluation. The threshold public key is determined by the constant-term commitment, and each participant public share is determined by evaluating the committed polynomial at that participant's evaluation point.

This specification uses the secp256k1 group and the ''[https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591#name-frostsecp256k1-sha-256 FROST(secp256k1, SHA-256)]'' ciphersuite defined by RFC 9591. Scalars are encoded as 32-byte big-endian integers in the canonical scalar encoding defined in [[#Encodings|Encodings]], and group elements are encoded using the 33-byte compressed secp256k1 point encoding defined in [[#Encodings|Encodings]]. The group public key is ''s * G'', where ''s'' is the threshold secret scalar and ''G'' is the base generator point of the secp256k1 curve. The public share for participant ''id'' is ''f(id + 1) * G'', where ''f'' is the underlying sharing polynomial.

The threshold secret MUST be a valid nonzero secp256k1 scalar. It MAY be generated by the dealer or supplied to the dealer as an existing secret scalar. A supplied secret MUST be generated using a cryptographically secure random number generator and MUST satisfy the entropy requirements specified by [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591 RFC 9591] (referencing [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc4086 RFC 4086]). When generating a new secret, the dealer MUST use a cryptographically secure random number generator.

This document specifies the key-generation inputs and outputs, Shamir polynomial construction, participant identifiers, VSS commitments, secret-share verification, public-share derivation, secret-share distribution, and failure conditions required to produce key material compatible with [BIP 445](https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing).

This document specifies key generation only. Signing, nonce generation, signature aggregation, and application-specific key tweaking are outside the scope of this document and are specified by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445] and the applicable Bitcoin specifications.

Trusted-dealer key generation requires the dealer to know the threshold secret during the key generation procedure. The dealer MUST be trusted to keep the secret and the secret shares confidential, and MUST delete them after the completion of key generation.  Secret shares MUST be distributed over mutually authenticated secure channels that provide confidentiality and integrity. Applications in which no single party is permitted to learn the threshold secret MUST use a distributed key generation protocol instead.

