=== Motivation ===

[https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591 RFC 9591] specifies the Flexible Round-Optimized Schnorr Threshold (FROST) signing protocol for prime-order groups. Key generation is explicitly outside the scope of the FROST signing protocol; however, [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591#name-trusted-dealer-key-generati Appendix C] describes a trusted-dealer construction based on Shamir secret sharing and Feldman verifiable secret sharing for generating and distributing FROST signing key shares. That construction is generic and is provided as a reference mechanism rather than as a Bitcoin-specific key-generation specification. [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445] specifies FROST signing for secp256k1 and BIP340-compatible Schnorr signatures. It permits implementations to obtain FROST key material either from a trusted-dealer construction, such as the one described in [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591#name-trusted-dealer-key-generati RFC 9591, Appendix C], or from a [https://github.com/BlockstreamResearch/bip-frost-dkg distributed key-generation protocol]. [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445] also defines the key material consumed by its signing protocol, including participant identifiers, participant public shares, and the threshold public key.

A Bitcoin implementation that chooses trusted-dealer key generation therefore requires additional rules that are not fixed by [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591 RFC 9591] or [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445]. In particular, an interoperable implementation needs concrete serialization rules for secp256k1 scalars and public points, key-generation parameters and validation requirements, secret-share verification and failure handling, and the representation of the resulting public and private key material. This specification fills that gap by specifying a Bitcoin-specific trusted-dealer key-generation procedure based on the Shamir and Feldman construction described in [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591#name-trusted-dealer-key-generati RFC 9591, Appendix C]. It defines the inputs and outputs of key generation and the relationship between the resulting key material and the [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing#signers-context FROST signing context] specified by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445]. The procedure is intended to produce key material that can be consumed by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445] without requiring a separate key-generation protocol.

Because FROST keys may be used as Taproot keys, the key-generation procedure also addresses the Taproot security requirements relevant to the resulting threshold key. [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0341.mediawiki BIP 341] recommends that a Taproot output with no script path commit to an unspendable script path, and the current [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445] specification requires key-generation protocols used with its signing protocol to account for this requirement. This specification therefore defines the necessary relationship between trusted-dealer key generation and the Taproot-safe threshold key material used by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445].

This specification defines a trusted-dealer scheme and does not provide the security properties of [https://github.com/BlockstreamResearch/bip-frost-dkg distributed key generation]. The dealer necessarily has knowledge of the complete threshold secret during key generation and must therefore be trusted to generate the key material correctly and protect the secret material. Applications in which no single party is permitted to learn the threshold secret MUST use a [https://github.com/BlockstreamResearch/bip-frost-dkg distributed key-generation] protocol instead.

This specification specifies key generation and its resulting key material only. The FROST signing protocol, nonce generation, signature-share generation, signature aggregation, and related signing procedures are specified by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445]. Bitcoin's Taproot consensus rules are specified by [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0341.mediawiki BIP 341]; this BIP only specifies the key-generation requirements necessary for the resulting FROST key material to be used consistently with those rules and with [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445].

==== Trusted Dealer vs. Distributed Key Generation ====

A trusted dealer is a key-generation mechanism in which one party generates the complete threshold secret, constructs the sharing polynomial, derives the secret shares, and distributes one share to each participant. The dealer therefore learns the complete threshold secret and MUST be trusted to generate the key material correctly and protect the secret shares during distribution. This construction is appropriate when the participants accept a trusted setup party, such as a provisioning service or a trusted hardware device, for key generation.

A distributed key-generation (DKG) protocol removes the requirement to trust a single party with the complete threshold secret by having the participants jointly generate the key material. Deployments that do not accept a trusted dealer as part of their security model SHOULD use a suitable DKG protocol, such as [https://github.com/BlockstreamResearch/bip-frost-dkg ChillDKG] instead.

This specification defines only the trusted-dealer construction. It does not define or replace a DKG protocol. The two approaches have different trust and security assumptions, but both can produce the FROST key material required by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445], including a threshold public key, participant public shares, and an individual secret share for each participant. [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445] defines the requirements for this key material and leaves its generation to a separate key-generation protocol. Applications MUST choose the key-generation mechanism according to their trust model. A trusted-dealer construction MUST NOT be used where the dealer is not permitted to learn the complete threshold secret. In such applications, a DKG protocol MUST be used instead.

== Specification ==

=== Overview ===

This specification defines a trusted-dealer procedure for generating the FROST key material required by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445] for FROST signing with secp256k1 and BIP340-compatible Schnorr signatures.

The procedure consists of the following stages:
* The dealer selects the key-generation parameters, including the total number of participants ''n'' and the signing threshold ''t''.
* The dealer obtains a valid threshold secret. The dealer MAY generate a new threshold secret using cryptographically secure randomness or, if supported by the implementation, accept an existing threshold secret subject to the validation requirements defined by this specification.
* The dealer constructs a Shamir secret-sharing polynomial whose constant term is the threshold secret and whose degree is at most ''t - 1''.
* Participant identifiers are assigned according to the BIP 445 convention ''id = 0, ..., n - 1''. The corresponding nonzero Shamir evaluation point for participant ''id'' is ''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''.
* The dealer evaluates the sharing polynomial at each participant's evaluation point and obtains one secret share for each participant.
* The dealer computes Feldman Verifiable Secret Sharing (VSS) commitments to the coefficients of the sharing polynomial.
* The key-generation material is transformed as required by the Taproot-safety procedure defined by this specification so that the final threshold public key satisfies the key-generation requirements of BIP 445. The final secret shares, participant public shares, threshold public key, and VSS commitment MUST remain mutually consistent after this transformation.
* The dealer distributes exactly one final secret share to each corresponding participant using a mutually authenticated channel that provides confidentiality and integrity. The VSS commitment is made available to all participants.
* All participants MUST establish a consistent view of the VSS commitment used for the key-generation procedure. A participant MUST NOT accept key material associated with a different or inconsistent VSS commitment.
* Each participant verifies its received secret share against the VSS commitment at its evaluation point ''id + 1''. A participant MUST reject the key-generation result if this verification fails.
* The threshold public key and participant public shares are derived from the same accepted VSS commitment. Each participant public share MUST correspond to that participant's accepted secret share.
* Upon successful completion of the key-generation procedure, the dealer MUST erase the threshold secret, polynomial coefficients, participant secret shares, and temporary copies of secret key-generation material that are no longer required, subject to the limitations of the execution environment.

A successful key-generation procedure produces public key-generation data consisting of:
''n'', the total number of participants;
''t'', the signing threshold;
''thresh_pk'', the threshold public key;
''pubshares'', containing the public share corresponding to every participant; and
''vss_commitment'', the Feldman VSS commitment associated with the sharing polynomial.

Each participant ''id'' additionally receives the private secret share ''secshare<sub>id</sub>''. The participant MUST keep this value confidential.

For every participant, the resulting key material MUST satisfy ''pubshare<sub>id</sub> = secshare<sub>id</sub>G''.

The participant identifiers, secret shares, participant public shares, and threshold public key MUST additionally satisfy the interpolation relationships required by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445]. Consequently, for any valid signer set containing at least ''t'' participants, interpolation of the corresponding participant public shares using the BIP 445 identifier-to-evaluation-point mapping MUST yield the same threshold public key.

The VSS commitment is public key-generation data used to verify the relationship between the sharing polynomial and the participant shares. It is not an input to a BIP 445 signing session.

This specification defines trusted-dealer key generation and the resulting key material only. FROST signing-session procedures are specified by BIP 445. Bitcoin's Taproot consensus and output-construction rules are not redefined by this specification; this specification defines only the key-generation requirements necessary for the resulting FROST key material to satisfy BIP 445.

=== Roles ===

This specification defines two roles involved in trusted-dealer key generation: the '''dealer''' and the '''participant'''.

; '''Dealer'''
: The dealer performs the trusted-dealer key-generation procedure. The dealer obtains the threshold secret according to this specification, constructs the Shamir sharing polynomial, computes the corresponding Feldman VSS commitments, derives one secret share for each participant, and distributes each share to its corresponding participant. Because the dealer necessarily has access to the complete threshold secret and all participant secret shares during key generation, the dealer MUST be trusted to generate the key material correctly and to protect all secret key-generation material while it is held.

; '''Participant'''
: A participant is one of the ''n'' parties for which key material is generated. Each participant is assigned a unique participant identifier ''id'', receives the secret share corresponding to that identifier, and verifies the share against the VSS commitment according to this specification. A participant MUST NOT accept or use a secret share that fails verification. An accepted secret share is long-term private key material and MUST remain confidential for as long as the corresponding threshold key remains in use.

A participant may subsequently be selected to take part in a FROST signing session. A participant selected for a particular signing session is referred to by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445] as a '''signer'''. Signer selection is part of the signing protocol and is distinct from key generation. Every signer is a participant, but not every participant necessarily takes part in a particular signing session.

The dealer is a key-generation role and is not required to participate in subsequent FROST signing sessions. After successful completion of key generation, subsequent signing requires the participants' accepted secret shares and the corresponding public key material; it does not require the dealer to retain the original threshold secret, polynomial coefficients, or copies of participant secret shares.
=== Parameters and Identifiers ===

The trusted-dealer key-generation procedure is parameterized by the total number of participants ''n'' and the configured threshold ''t''.

The parameters MUST satisfy:

:''1 ≤ t ≤ n < 2<sup>32</sup>''.

Here:

* ''n'' is the total number of participants for which key material is generated; and
* ''t'' is the configured threshold used by the resulting FROST key material.

The case ''n = 1'' is permitted. In this case, ''t = 1'', the sole participant holds the complete signing secret represented by its secret share, and the configuration provides no threshold security. An implementation MAY instead use an ordinary BIP340 key pair for this case.

==== Participant Identifiers ====

Each participant is assigned a unique integer identifier ''id'' satisfying

:''0 ≤ id < n''.

For a key-generation procedure with ''n'' participants, the complete set of participant identifiers is therefore

:''{0, 1, ..., n - 1}''.

These identifiers are the participant identifiers used by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445]. A participant identifier is distinct from the Shamir evaluation point associated with that participant and MUST NOT be used directly as the polynomial evaluation point.

==== Shamir Evaluation Points ====

For participant ''id'', define the corresponding Shamir evaluation point as

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''.

Thus, participant identifiers

:''0, 1, ..., n - 1''

correspond respectively to the Shamir evaluation points

:''1, 2, ..., n''.

The evaluation point ''x<sub>id</sub>'' is interpreted as a scalar in the secp256k1 scalar field. For every valid participant identifier,

:''1 ≤ x<sub>id</sub> ≤ n < 2<sup>32</sup> < q'',

where ''q'' is the order of the secp256k1 generator. Consequently, every participant evaluation point is nonzero, and different participant identifiers correspond to distinct scalar evaluation points.

The mapping

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''

is required for compatibility with BIP 445. BIP 445 uses zero-based participant identifiers while its Lagrange interpolation interprets those identifiers as the corresponding nonzero points obtained by adding one.

The distinction between ''id'' and ''x<sub>id</sub>'' MUST be preserved:

* ''id'' is the zero-based participant identifier used in BIP 445 data structures and protocol inputs;
* ''x<sub>id</sub>'' is the nonzero scalar used for Shamir polynomial evaluation and interpolation; and
* the secret share belonging to participant ''id'' is obtained by evaluating the sharing polynomial at ''x<sub>id</sub>''.

In particular,

:''x<sub>0</sub> = 1''

and

:''x<sub>n-1</sub> = n''.

==== Threshold and Polynomial Degree ====

Let the sharing polynomial be

:''f(x) = a<sub>0</sub> + a<sub>1</sub>x + ... + a<sub>t-1</sub>x<sup>t-1</sup>''

over the secp256k1 scalar field, with degree at most ''t - 1''.

The constant coefficient ''a<sub>0</sub>'' is the secret shared by the polynomial. The secret share assigned to participant ''id'' is

:''secshare<sub>id</sub> = f(x<sub>id</sub>) = f(id + 1)''.

All polynomial arithmetic is performed modulo the secp256k1 group order ''q'', as defined in [[#Cryptographic Domain|Cryptographic Domain]].

The secret represented by ''a<sub>0</sub>'' and every participant secret share produced by a successful key-generation procedure MUST be valid nonzero secp256k1 scalars. A key-generation attempt that produces a zero participant secret share MUST NOT be accepted. The retry procedure for such an attempt is defined in [[#Polynomial Generation|Polynomial Generation]].

==== Relationship to BIP 445 ====

The parameters and identifiers defined by this specification map to BIP 445 as follows:

* ''n'' is the total number of participants involved in key generation;
* ''t'' is the configured signing threshold;
* ''id'' is the BIP 445 participant identifier;
* ''secshare<sub>id</sub>'' is the long-term secret share held by participant ''id'';
* ''pubshare<sub>id</sub>'' is the public share corresponding to ''secshare<sub>id</sub>''; and
* ''thresh_pk'' is the final threshold public key supplied to BIP 445.

A BIP 445 signing session selects ''u'' distinct participants to act as signers, where

:''t ≤ u ≤ n''.

The value ''u'' is a signing-session parameter and is not an input to the trusted-dealer key-generation procedure.

The key-generation procedure MUST produce participant identifiers, secret shares, public shares, and a threshold public key that remain mutually consistent for every valid signer set accepted by BIP 445. In particular, the public shares of a valid signer set MUST interpolate to the same ''thresh_pk'' using BIP 445's ''id + 1'' interpolation mapping.
=== Cryptographic Domain ===

This specification uses the secp256k1 group and scalar domain defined by the [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591#name-frostsecp256k1-sha-256 FROST(secp256k1, SHA-256)] ciphersuite specified by [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591 RFC 9591]. This specification does not adopt or redefine the FROST signing algorithms or hash functions of RFC 9591; signing of the resulting key material is specified by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445].

Let ''G'' denote the standard generator of the secp256k1 group and let ''q'' denote its prime order:

:''q = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFEBAAEDCE6AF48A03BBFD25E8CD0364141''.

Scalars are elements of the field of integers modulo ''q''. Unless otherwise stated, all scalar addition, subtraction, multiplication, exponentiation, and inversion in this specification are performed modulo ''q''. Scalar inversion is defined only for nonzero scalars.

The point at infinity is the identity element of the secp256k1 group and is denoted by ''inf_point''.

A scalar used as a threshold secret or participant secret share MUST be nonzero and therefore MUST satisfy

:''1 ≤ s < q''.

A scalar used internally as a polynomial coefficient MAY be zero unless a later rule explicitly requires otherwise.

Let the sharing polynomial be

:''f(x) = a<sub>0</sub> + a<sub>1</sub>x + ... + a<sub>t-1</sub>x<sup>t-1</sup>''

over the scalar field, where

* ''a<sub>0</sub>'' is the secret shared by the polynomial; and
* ''a<sub>1</sub>, ..., a<sub>t-1</sub>'' are the non-constant polynomial coefficients.

The polynomial has degree at most ''t - 1''. A non-constant coefficient MAY be zero.

For participant ''id'', let

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''

be the participant's Shamir evaluation point. The corresponding secret share is

:''secshare<sub>id</sub> = f(x<sub>id</sub>) mod q''.

Every secret share produced by a successful key-generation procedure MUST be nonzero. Its corresponding participant public share is

:''pubshare<sub>id</sub> = secshare<sub>id</sub>G''.

Consequently, an accepted participant public share MUST NOT be ''inf_point''.

==== Feldman VSS Commitments ====

For every polynomial coefficient ''a<sub>j</sub>'', define the corresponding Feldman commitment

:''A<sub>j</sub> = a<sub>j</sub>G''

for

:''0 ≤ j < t''.

The ordered sequence

:''(A<sub>0</sub>, A<sub>1</sub>, ..., A<sub>t-1</sub>)''

is the Feldman VSS commitment to the sharing polynomial.

The constant-term commitment is

:''A<sub>0</sub> = a<sub>0</sub>G''.

Because ''a<sub>0</sub>'' is required to be nonzero, ''A<sub>0</sub>'' MUST NOT be ''inf_point''.

A non-constant coefficient ''a<sub>j</sub>'' MAY be zero. In that case,

:''A<sub>j</sub> = inf_point''.

Therefore, the point at infinity MUST be permitted for non-constant Feldman commitments. The encoding of such commitments is defined in [[#Encodings|Encodings]].

For participant ''id'', let ''x = x<sub>id</sub>''. Evaluation of the committed polynomial in the group gives

:''P<sub>id</sub> =
A<sub>0</sub> +
A<sub>1</sub>x +
... +
A<sub>t-1</sub>x<sup>t-1</sup>''.

Scalar multiplication of group elements uses scalars modulo ''q''. For a correctly generated share,

:''P<sub>id</sub> = secshare<sub>id</sub>G = pubshare<sub>id</sub>''.

Thus, verification of a participant secret share consists of establishing that scalar evaluation of the sharing polynomial and group evaluation of the corresponding Feldman commitment produce consistent results.

The point at infinity MUST NOT be accepted as:

* the threshold public key; or
* a participant public share.

The point at infinity MAY occur as a non-constant Feldman commitment when the corresponding polynomial coefficient is zero.

Feldman commitments allow a participant to verify that its secret share is consistent with the polynomial committed to by the dealer. They do not hide the committed coefficients information-theoretically, although recovering a coefficient from its commitment requires solving the discrete logarithm problem in secp256k1.

Share verification does not establish that the dealer generated the threshold secret or polynomial coefficients using unbiased randomness, maintained confidentiality of secret material, erased secret material after distribution, or refrained from distributing additional copies of secret shares. Those properties remain part of the trusted-dealer assumption.

==== Group and Scalar Operations ====

For a scalar ''a'' and group element ''P'', ''aP'' denotes scalar multiplication. For group elements ''P'' and ''Q'', ''P + Q'' denotes elliptic-curve group addition.

Scalar expressions are evaluated modulo ''q''. Group expressions are evaluated in the secp256k1 group, whose identity element is ''inf_point''.

Implementations MUST validate externally supplied encoded group elements according to the type of value being parsed.

A threshold public key or participant public share MUST decode to a valid secp256k1 point other than ''inf_point''.

A Feldman VSS commitment element MUST decode according to the VSS commitment encoding defined by this specification. The constant-term commitment ''A<sub>0</sub>'' MUST NOT be ''inf_point'', while a non-constant commitment MAY be ''inf_point'' when the corresponding coefficient is zero.

Because secp256k1 has cofactor one, successful validation that an encoded non-infinity point lies on the secp256k1 curve is sufficient to establish membership in the prime-order group used by this specification.

==== Relationship to the FROST Ciphersuite ====

The group, generator, group order, scalar arithmetic, and ordinary compressed-point representation used by this specification are derived from the FROST(secp256k1, SHA-256) ciphersuite specified by RFC 9591.

This specification does not define a new FROST ciphersuite and does not modify the BIP 445 signing algorithms. It defines the trusted-dealer key-generation procedure and the representations required for the resulting Bitcoin-compatible FROST key material.

The resulting participant secret shares, participant public shares, and threshold public key are intended to be supplied to BIP 445 subject to the validation requirements specified there.

=== Public-Key Representations ===

The threshold public key ''thresh_pk'' and participant public shares ''pubshare<sub>id</sub>'' produced by this specification are represented as ordinary 33-byte compressed secp256k1 public keys. The serialization and parsing rules for these values are defined in [[#Encodings|Encodings]].

This representation is the plain public-key representation consumed by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445]. It is distinct from the 32-byte x-only public-key representation used by [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0340.mediawiki BIP 340].

For a non-infinity secp256k1 point

:''P = (x, y)'',

the compressed representation contains the parity of ''y'' together with the 32-byte X coordinate. Consequently, the compressed encodings of ''P'' and ''-P'' are distinct.

The x-only representation of ''P'' consists only of the 32-byte X coordinate:

:''xbytes(P) = bytes(x(P))''.

Because ''P'' and ''-P'' have the same X coordinate,

:''xbytes(P) = xbytes(-P)''.

When a 32-byte x-only public key is interpreted according to BIP340, it denotes the unique secp256k1 point having the encoded X coordinate and an even Y coordinate. Thus, if ''P'' has even Y, its x-only representation denotes ''P''; if ''P'' has odd Y, the same x-only representation denotes ''-P''.

For an ordinary compressed public-key encoding

:''cbytes(P) = prefix || xbytes(P)'',

the x-only serialization is therefore equal to the final 32 bytes of the compressed encoding:

:''xbytes(P) = cbytes(P)[1:33]''.

Discarding the prefix byte produces the correct x-only serialization, but also discards the Y-parity information contained in the compressed representation. Implementations MUST account for the even-Y interpretation required by BIP340 whenever the corresponding group element or secret scalar is used in an operation whose semantics depend on the full point.

This specification does not require participant public shares or the threshold public key to have even Y coordinates. They remain ordinary full secp256k1 points represented in compressed form. Any parity adjustment required for BIP340-compatible threshold signing is performed according to BIP 445.

The ''thresh_pk'' output by this key-generation procedure is therefore a plain 33-byte compressed threshold public key in the representation required by BIP 445. Although the underlying threshold key MUST satisfy the Taproot-safety requirements imposed by BIP 445, ''thresh_pk'' is not itself a Taproot output key or a Taproot witness program.

When an x-only representation of the threshold public key or a tweaked threshold public key is required, implementations MUST apply the conversion and tweaking rules specified by BIP 445 and the applicable Bitcoin specification. Taproot output construction, including derivation of the BIP341 output key, is outside the scope of this section.

=== Encodings ===

Unless otherwise specified, integer and scalar encodings defined by this specification use big-endian byte order.

==== Scalar Encoding ====

A scalar is encoded as exactly 32 bytes containing its unsigned big-endian integer representation.

For a general scalar ''s'', the encoding is valid if and only if

:''0 ≤ s < q''.

Thus, the all-zero 32-byte string is the canonical encoding of the zero scalar.

Some values in this specification are required to be nonzero. In a context requiring a nonzero scalar, the encoded value MUST instead satisfy

:''1 ≤ s < q''.

In particular, threshold secrets and participant secret shares MUST use nonzero scalar encodings. The all-zero encoding MUST therefore be rejected when parsing either of those values.

An encoded scalar whose numerical value is greater than or equal to ''q'' MUST be rejected unless an algorithm explicitly specifies modular reduction. Key-generation secret values defined by this specification MUST NOT be made valid by reducing an invalid external encoding modulo ''q''.

Scalar encodings are canonical: every scalar in the range ''0 <= s < q'' has exactly one valid 32-byte encoding.

==== Compressed Point Encoding ====

For a secp256k1 point ''P = (X, Y)'' other than the point at infinity, define ''cbytes(P)'' as the 33-byte compressed SEC1 encoding:

* ''0x02 || X'' if ''Y'' is even; and
* ''0x03 || X'' if ''Y'' is odd,

where ''X'' is encoded as exactly 32 bytes in big-endian byte order.

The corresponding parser ''cpoint(b)'' accepts only a 33-byte canonical compressed encoding that decodes to a valid non-infinity secp256k1 point.

An ordinary compressed point encoding MUST be rejected if:

* its length is not exactly 33 bytes;
* its prefix is neither ''0x02'' nor ''0x03'';
* its X coordinate does not correspond to a point on secp256k1; or
* it otherwise fails secp256k1 public-key validation.

The point at infinity has no valid ''cbytes'' encoding.

The threshold public key ''thresh_pk'' and every participant public share ''pubshare<sub>id</sub>'' MUST use this ordinary compressed-point representation and MUST therefore represent non-infinity points.

==== Extended Point Encoding ====

For values that are permitted to contain the point at infinity, this specification defines the extended compressed-point encoding ''cbytes_ext''.

For a non-infinity point ''P'':

:''cbytes_ext(P) = cbytes(P)''.

For the point at infinity:

:''cbytes_ext(inf_point) = 0x00<sup>33</sup>''.

That is, the point at infinity is represented by exactly 33 zero bytes.

The corresponding parser ''cpoint_ext(b)'' behaves as follows:

* if ''b'' is exactly 33 zero bytes, return ''inf_point'';
* otherwise, parse ''b'' using ''cpoint'';
* fail if neither case succeeds.

The all-zero representation is unambiguous because an ordinary compressed secp256k1 point encoding begins with either ''0x02'' or ''0x03''.

The extended encoding MUST be used only where this specification explicitly permits the point at infinity. In particular, it is used for serialized Feldman VSS commitment elements. It MUST NOT be used to permit an infinity value where this specification requires an ordinary public key.

==== Participant Identifier Encoding ====

A participant identifier ''id'' is an integer satisfying

:''0 ≤ id < 2<sup>32</sup>''.

When serialized, ''id'' is encoded as exactly four bytes containing its unsigned big-endian integer representation:

:''id_bytes = bytes(4, id)''.

For a key-generation procedure with ''n'' participants, only identifiers satisfying

:''0 ≤ id < n''

are valid.

The serialized participant identifier MUST NOT be confused with its Shamir evaluation point. The participant identifier is the four-byte encoding of ''id'', whereas the corresponding Shamir evaluation point is the scalar

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''.

==== VSS Commitment Encoding ====

For threshold ''t'', let

:''(A<sub>0</sub>, A<sub>1</sub>, ..., A<sub>t-1</sub>)''

be the ordered Feldman commitments to the sharing-polynomial coefficients.

The serialized VSS commitment is

:''vss_commitment =
cbytes_ext(A<sub>0</sub>) ||
cbytes_ext(A<sub>1</sub>) ||
... ||
cbytes_ext(A<sub>t-1</sub>)''.

The serialized VSS commitment therefore has length exactly

:''33t''

bytes.

Commitment elements MUST appear in increasing coefficient order. The element ''A<sub>j</sub>'' MUST correspond to coefficient ''a<sub>j</sub>''.

When parsing a VSS commitment:

* ''t'' MUST be at least 1;
* the input MUST contain exactly ''33t'' bytes;
* each 33-byte element MUST successfully decode using ''cpoint_ext'';
* ''A<sub>0</sub>'' MUST NOT be ''inf_point''; and
* a non-constant commitment ''A<sub>j</sub>'' for ''j > 0'' MAY be ''inf_point''.

An infinity non-constant commitment represents a zero polynomial coefficient:

:''A<sub>j</sub> = inf_point iff a<sub>j</sub> = 0''

for a correctly generated commitment.

The order of commitment elements is significant. Implementations MUST NOT reorder, omit, duplicate, insert, or otherwise modify commitment elements when interpreting a serialized VSS commitment.

The VSS commitment is public key-generation data used to verify participant shares. It is not an input to a BIP 445 signing session and MUST NOT be confused with the participant public-share list used by BIP 445.

==== Key-Generation Outputs ====

A successful key-generation procedure produces the following public data:

* ''n'', the total number of participants;
* ''t'', the configured threshold;
* ''thresh_pk'', the 33-byte ordinary compressed threshold public key;
* ''pubshares[id]'', the 33-byte ordinary compressed public share for every participant ''id''; and
* ''vss_commitment'', the ''33t''-byte serialized Feldman VSS commitment.

For each participant ''id'', the key-generation procedure additionally produces:

* ''id'', the participant's public identifier; and
* ''secshare[id]'', the participant's private 32-byte nonzero scalar secret share.

Only ''secshare[id]'' is secret. The participant MUST keep this value confidential.

The threshold public key, participant identifiers, participant public shares, and VSS commitment are public key-generation data. Their publication does not require confidentiality, although implementations MUST preserve their integrity and ensure that participants agree on the key-generation data associated with a particular ceremony.

The VSS commitment is retained for key-generation verification and related application purposes. It is not required as an input by the BIP 445 signing algorithms once the necessary BIP 445 key material has been established.

=== Key-Generation State ===

The dealer maintains both secret and public state while performing the trusted-dealer key-generation procedure.

==== Secret State ====

Secret state includes all values whose disclosure would reveal the shared secret, a polynomial coefficient, or a participant secret share.

In particular, secret state includes:

* the constant coefficient ''a<sub>0</sub>'' of the sharing polynomial;
* all non-constant polynomial coefficients ''a<sub>1</sub>, ..., a<sub>t-1</sub>'';
* every participant secret share generated during the procedure;
* any intermediate or transformed secret shares produced by key-generation operations;
* secret random values or accepted random-sampling material from which any of the preceding values can be recovered; and
* temporary, serialized, or otherwise duplicated representations of any secret value.

The dealer MUST treat all secret state as confidential for the entire period during which it exists.

Secret state MUST NOT be transmitted, logged, persisted, exposed through diagnostics, or made available to a party other than as explicitly required by this specification. In particular, each participant MUST receive only the secret share intended for that participant.

Upon successful completion of the key-generation procedure, the dealer MUST delete all secret state that is no longer required, including the sharing-polynomial coefficients, dealer-retained copies of participant secret shares, and temporary secret values.

Logical deletion requires that the implementation no longer intentionally retain or use those values. Implementations SHOULD additionally use cryptographic secret-memory and zeroization mechanisms where available to minimize recoverable copies of secret material in memory.

An implementation running in an environment that cannot guarantee physical memory erasure cannot in general guarantee that every historical copy of a secret value has been overwritten. This limitation does not remove the requirement to avoid retaining secret key-generation state after it is no longer required.

Subsequent FROST signing MUST NOT depend on retention of the dealer's secret key-generation state. Once key generation has completed successfully and each participant has obtained its accepted secret share, the dealer's copies of the sharing polynomial and participant secret shares are not required by the BIP 445 signing protocol.

==== Public State ====

Public key-generation state includes:

* ''n'', the total number of participants;
* ''t'', the configured threshold;
* ''thresh_pk'', the threshold public key;
* ''pubshares'', the participant public shares; and
* ''vss_commitment'', the Feldman VSS commitment.

Public state does not require confidentiality and MAY be retained after completion of key generation.

Although public state is not secret, implementations MUST preserve its integrity. Participants MUST NOT accept public key-generation data that has been substituted, modified, or associated with a different key-generation ceremony.

The mechanisms by which participants establish a consistent view of the public key-generation state are specified by the applicable distribution and participant-acceptance rules of this specification.

=== Threshold Secret Generation and Import ===

The trusted-dealer key-generation procedure requires an input threshold secret represented by a valid nonzero secp256k1 scalar. The secret MAY be generated by the key-generation implementation or, if supported, supplied by the application as an existing secret.

==== Imported Threshold Secret ====

An implementation MAY support importing an existing threshold secret.

An imported threshold secret MUST be encoded as exactly 32 bytes using the canonical scalar encoding defined in [[#Scalar Encoding|Scalar Encoding]]. The encoded integer MUST satisfy

:''1 ≤ s < q''.

If the encoding is not exactly 32 bytes, represents zero, or represents an integer greater than or equal to ''q'', key generation MUST fail.

An implementation MUST NOT modify an invalid imported encoding in order to obtain a valid threshold secret. In particular, it MUST NOT hash, reduce modulo ''q'', truncate, clamp, increment, normalize, or otherwise transform an invalid input for the purpose of making that input valid.

This requirement applies to parsing and validation of the imported secret. It does not prohibit cryptographic transformations that are explicitly required by later stages of this specification after the imported value has first been accepted as a valid nonzero scalar.

The key-generation implementation cannot determine the provenance or entropy of an arbitrary imported secret from its scalar encoding alone. The application supplying an imported secret is therefore responsible for ensuring that the secret was generated or derived securely.

Consistent with the input requirements of the trusted-dealer construction in [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591#appendix-C RFC 9591 Appendix C], an imported threshold secret MUST be derived from at least ''Ns'' bytes of entropy. For the FROST(secp256k1, SHA-256) ciphersuite, ''Ns = 32''. The application supplying the secret is responsible for satisfying this requirement because it cannot be verified from the imported scalar itself.

Importing an existing secret does not increase its entropy or repair weaknesses in its original generation, derivation, storage, or handling.

==== Generated Threshold Secret ====

When the dealer generates a new threshold secret, it MUST use a cryptographically secure source of randomness.

The generated secret MUST be uniformly sampled from the nonzero secp256k1 scalar values:

:''s ∈ {1, ..., q - 1}''.

An implementation MAY generate such a value by repeatedly invoking a uniform scalar-generation procedure over ''{0, ..., q - 1}'' until the result is nonzero.

Random scalar generation SHOULD follow one of the methods specified in [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9591#appendix-D RFC 9591 Appendix D], such as rejection sampling or wide reduction using sufficient random input.

An implementation MUST NOT obtain a purportedly uniform threshold secret merely by interpreting 32 uniformly random bytes as an integer and reducing that integer modulo ''q'', because naive reduction in this manner does not in general produce a uniform distribution over the scalar field.

If the scalar-generation procedure produces zero, the implementation MUST discard that value and sample again.

The accepted threshold secret becomes the constant coefficient of the initial Shamir sharing polynomial:

:''a<sub>0</sub> = s''.

Any transformation of the resulting sharing required by a later stage of this specification MUST be performed only after ''s'' has been accepted as a valid threshold-secret input.

=== Polynomial Generation ===

Let ''a<sub>0</sub>'' be the valid nonzero threshold-secret input accepted according to [[#Threshold Secret Generation and Import|Threshold Secret Generation and Import]].

For threshold ''t = 1'', the sharing polynomial consists only of the constant coefficient:

:''f(x) = a<sub>0</sub>''.

Because ''a<sub>0</sub>'' is nonzero, every participant evaluation of this degree-zero polynomial is also nonzero.

For ''t > 1'', the dealer independently samples ''t - 1'' non-constant scalar coefficients:

:''a<sub>1</sub>, ..., a<sub>t-1</sub>''.

Each non-constant coefficient MUST be sampled independently and uniformly from the secp256k1 scalar field:

:''a<sub>j</sub> ∈ {0, ..., q - 1}''.

The dealer MUST use a cryptographically secure uniform scalar-generation procedure consistent with the requirements of [[#Threshold Secret Generation and Import|Threshold Secret Generation and Import]] and RFC 9591.

A non-constant coefficient MAY be zero. In particular, the highest listed coefficient ''a<sub>t-1</sub>'' is not required to be nonzero. Consequently, the resulting polynomial has degree at most ''t - 1''.

The sharing polynomial is

:''f(x) =
a<sub>0</sub> +
a<sub>1</sub>x +
... +
a<sub>t-1</sub>x<sup>t-1</sup> mod q''.

For every participant identifier

:''id ∈ {0, ..., n - 1}'',

the dealer computes the corresponding Shamir evaluation point

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''

and evaluates

:''d<sub>id</sub> = f(x<sub>id</sub>) mod q''.

A polynomial-generation attempt is acceptable only if

:''d<sub>id</sub> != 0''

for every participant.

If any participant evaluation produces the zero scalar, the dealer MUST discard the complete set of non-constant coefficients

:''a<sub>1</sub>, ..., a<sub>t-1</sub>''

from that attempt and sample a fresh independent set of non-constant coefficients. The dealer MUST retain the already accepted constant coefficient ''a<sub>0</sub>'' while performing this retry.

The dealer MUST NOT repair a zero participant evaluation by independently modifying the resulting share, changing the corresponding public share, reducing or incrementing the share, or otherwise altering an individual participant's output. All participant shares MUST remain evaluations of one common sharing polynomial.

For ''t > 1'', the dealer repeats non-constant coefficient generation and evaluation until every participant evaluation is nonzero or the implementation aborts the key-generation operation because of an internal failure condition.

No coefficient, share, commitment, or other key material from a rejected polynomial-generation attempt MAY be distributed to participants or used as key-generation output.

The resulting accepted coefficient vector is

:''coeffs =
[a<sub>0</sub>, a<sub>1</sub>, ..., a<sub>t-1</sub>]''.

The corresponding initial participant secret share is

:''secshare<sub>id</sub> =
f(id + 1)''.

The nonzero-share requirement in this section applies to the sharing produced at this stage. If a subsequent key-generation transformation modifies the shared secret or participant shares, the resulting final participant secret shares MUST again be validated as nonzero before they are accepted as BIP 445 key material.

=== Feldman Commitment Generation ===

After accepting a sharing polynomial

:''f(x) =
a<sub>0</sub> +
a<sub>1</sub>x +
... +
a<sub>t-1</sub>x<sup>t-1</sup>'',

the dealer computes a Feldman commitment to each coefficient:

:''A<sub>j</sub> = a<sub>j</sub>G''

for

:''0 ≤ j < t''.

The ordered commitment vector is

:''initial_vss_commitment =
(A<sub>0</sub>, A<sub>1</sub>, ..., A<sub>t-1</sub>)''.

The first commitment is the commitment to the constant coefficient:

:''A<sub>0</sub> = a<sub>0</sub>G''.

Because ''a<sub>0</sub>'' is required to be nonzero,

:''A<sub>0</sub> != inf_point''.

A non-constant polynomial coefficient MAY be zero. Consequently, for ''j > 0'',

:''a<sub>j</sub> = 0''

implies

:''A<sub>j</sub> = inf_point''.

The dealer MUST therefore permit ''inf_point'' as a non-constant Feldman commitment.

The canonical byte serialization of the initial commitment vector is

:''initial_vss_commitment_bytes =
cbytes_ext(A<sub>0</sub>) ||
cbytes_ext(A<sub>1</sub>) ||
... ||
cbytes_ext(A<sub>t-1</sub>)''.

This serialization has length exactly ''33t'' bytes.

Because ''A<sub>0</sub>'' is non-infinity,

:''cbytes_ext(A<sub>0</sub>) = cbytes(A<sub>0</sub>)''.

Each commitment element MUST remain in coefficient order. ''A<sub>j</sub>'' MUST correspond to ''a<sub>j</sub>'', and implementations MUST NOT reorder, omit, duplicate, or insert commitment elements.

The commitment generated in this section commits to the sharing polynomial produced by [[#Polynomial Generation|Polynomial Generation]]. If a subsequent key-generation step defined by this specification transforms the shared secret or sharing polynomial, the corresponding Feldman commitment MUST be transformed consistently before it becomes the final ''vss_commitment''.

In particular, the initial constant-term commitment ''A<sub>0</sub>'' MUST NOT be assumed to be the final BIP 445 ''thresh_pk'' until all key-generation transformations required by this specification have completed.

The final threshold public key MUST be derived from the constant-term commitment of the final accepted VSS commitment. The dealer MUST NOT generate or substitute an independent threshold public key unrelated to that commitment.

The dealer MUST use one canonical commitment value for the key-generation ceremony. Different participants MUST NOT intentionally be assigned different VSS commitments for the same ceremony. The requirements by which participants establish a consistent view of the final VSS commitment are defined in [[#Secret-Share Distribution|Secret-Share Distribution]] and [[#Participant Acceptance|Participant Acceptance]].

=== Participant Share Generation ===

After the dealer has accepted the sharing polynomial and computed its initial Feldman commitment, it derives one initial secret share and corresponding initial public share for every participant.

For each participant identifier

:''id ∈ {0, ..., n - 1}'',

the dealer computes the Shamir evaluation point

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''

and evaluates the sharing polynomial:

:''d<sub>id</sub> = f(x<sub>id</sub>) mod q''.

The value ''d<sub>id</sub>'' is the initial secret share corresponding to participant ''id''.

The dealer MUST verify that

:''1 ≤ d<sub>id</sub> < q''.

Because [[#Polynomial Generation|Polynomial Generation]] rejects any polynomial that evaluates to zero at a participant evaluation point, failure of this condition indicates that the accepted polynomial or its evaluation is inconsistent with the requirements of this specification. The dealer MUST abort the current key-generation attempt if this condition is not satisfied.

The dealer computes the corresponding initial participant public share

:''D<sub>id</sub> = d<sub>id</sub>G''.

The resulting point MUST NOT be ''inf_point''.

The dealer MUST additionally evaluate the initial Feldman commitment at the same participant evaluation point:

:''P<sub>id</sub> =
A<sub>0</sub> +
x<sub>id</sub>A<sub>1</sub> +
x<sub>id</sub><sup>2</sup>A<sub>2</sub> +
... +
x<sub>id</sub><sup>t-1</sup>A<sub>t-1</sub>''.

The dealer MUST verify

:''P<sub>id</sub> = D<sub>id</sub>''.

This equality follows from

:''A<sub>j</sub> = a<sub>j</sub>G''

and therefore establishes that scalar evaluation of the sharing polynomial and group evaluation of the corresponding Feldman commitment are consistent:

:''P<sub>id</sub>
= (Σ<sub>j=0</sub><sup>t-1</sup>
a<sub>j</sub>x<sub>id</sub><sup>j</sup>)G
= f(x<sub>id</sub>)G
= d<sub>id</sub>G''.

If the equality does not hold, the dealer MUST abort the key-generation attempt. The dealer MUST NOT repair the mismatch by modifying an individual secret share, public share, participant identifier, evaluation point, or commitment element.

The dealer performs these checks for every participant before any secret share is distributed.

The values ''d<sub>id</sub>'' and ''D<sub>id</sub>'' defined in this section are initial key-generation values associated with the initial sharing polynomial and initial VSS commitment. If a subsequent key-generation transformation defined by this specification modifies the shared secret, the dealer MUST transform the participant secret shares, participant public shares, and VSS commitment consistently.

Only after all required key-generation transformations and validation have completed do the resulting values become the final

:''secshare<sub>id</sub>''

and

:''pubshare<sub>id</sub>''

supplied to BIP 445.

For every final participant share, the key-generation procedure MUST preserve

:''pubshare<sub>id</sub> = secshare<sub>id</sub>G''

and the final participant public share MUST also equal evaluation of the final VSS commitment at ''x<sub>id</sub>''.

=== Secret-Share Distribution ===

After all required key-generation transformations and dealer-side validation have completed, the dealer distributes the final secret shares to the participants.

For every participant identifier

:''id ∈ {0, ..., n - 1}'',

the participant MUST be assigned exactly one final secret-share value

:''secshare<sub>id</sub>''.

The secret share distributed to participant ''id'' MUST be the share corresponding to that participant's evaluation point

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''

under the final accepted sharing polynomial.

The dealer MUST NOT distribute one participant's secret share as the secret share of another participant. Retransmission of the same secret-share value to its intended participant does not constitute generation of a new share.

The secret-share transport mechanism is application-specific and is not otherwise defined by this specification. The mechanism used to deliver a secret share MUST provide:

* confidentiality of the secret share;
* integrity of the transmitted secret share and associated key-generation data;
* authentication of the dealer;
* authentication of the intended participant; and
* binding of the secret share to the key-generation data for which it was generated.

These requirements are consistent with the mutually authenticated secure channel required for trusted-dealer key generation by RFC 9591.

For participant ''id'', the secret share MUST be authenticated together with a distribution context containing at least:

* ''n'';
* ''t'';
* the participant identifier ''id''; and
* the final ''vss_commitment''.

Conceptually,

:''distribution_context =
(n, t, id, vss_commitment)''.

This specification does not define a byte serialization or transport protocol for ''distribution_context''. An application MAY additionally bind an application-defined ceremony identifier, protocol transcript identifier, device identity, network identity, or other contextual information to the authenticated distribution record.

The final ''vss_commitment'' is public data and does not require confidentiality. However, its integrity and association with the distributed secret share MUST be protected.

Each participant MUST obtain the same final ''vss_commitment'' for a given key-generation result. Participants MUST NOT accept the key-generation result if they cannot establish a consistent view of this commitment. A secure authenticated broadcast mechanism MAY be used to establish such a common view, but this specification does not require a particular broadcast protocol.

For participant ''id'', the distributed secret share and final VSS commitment MUST satisfy the verification relationship defined in [[#Secret-Share Verification|Secret-Share Verification]].

A participant MUST NOT accept a secret share solely because it is associated with the expected participant identifier. In particular, a share from a different key-generation result MUST NOT be treated as belonging to the current key-generation result merely because the same ''id'' is used.

The dealer MUST NOT distribute initial or intermediate secret shares that precede any mandatory key-generation transformation. Only the final ''secshare<sub>id</sub>'' corresponding to the final ''vss_commitment'' and final threshold public key may be accepted as output of this specification.

After successful distribution and participant acceptance, dealer-held copies of participant secret shares are subject to the deletion requirements in [[#Key-Generation State|Key-Generation State]].

=== Participant Acceptance ===

A participant MUST NOT accept key-generation output until it has validated the key-generation parameters, its participant identifier, the final VSS commitment, and its final secret share, and has successfully verified the secret share against that commitment.

For participant ''id'', let the received key-generation data include at least:

* ''n'';
* ''t'';
* the participant identifier ''id'';
* the final ''vss_commitment''; and
* the participant's final ''secshare<sub>id</sub>''.

The participant MUST first verify that

:''1 ≤ t ≤ n < 2<sup>32</sup>''

and

:''0 ≤ id < n''.

The participant MUST verify that the serialized ''vss_commitment'' has length exactly

:''33t''

bytes and parses into exactly ''t'' ordered commitment elements:

:''(A<sub>0</sub>, A<sub>1</sub>, ..., A<sub>t-1</sub>)''.

Each commitment element MUST decode according to [[#Extended Point Encoding|Extended Point Encoding]].

The constant-term commitment

:''A<sub>0</sub>''

MUST be a valid non-infinity secp256k1 point.

For each

:''j > 0'',

the commitment ''A<sub>j</sub>'' MAY be ''inf_point''. An infinity non-constant commitment represents a zero polynomial coefficient and MUST NOT by itself cause participant rejection.

The received secret share MUST be exactly 32 bytes and MUST decode as a canonical nonzero scalar satisfying

:''1 ≤ secshare<sub>id</sub> < q''.

The participant MUST NOT reduce, truncate, hash, clamp, normalize, or otherwise modify an invalid secret-share encoding in order to make it acceptable.

The participant MUST then compute the corresponding Shamir evaluation point

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''

and the public point derived directly from its secret share:

:''S<sub>id</sub> = secshare<sub>id</sub>G''.

The participant MUST also evaluate the accepted VSS commitment at the same evaluation point:

:''V<sub>id</sub> =
A<sub>0</sub> +
x<sub>id</sub>A<sub>1</sub> +
x<sub>id</sub><sup>2</sup>A<sub>2</sub> +
... +
x<sub>id</sub><sup>t-1</sup>A<sub>t-1</sub>''.

The secret share is valid only if

:''S<sub>id</sub> = V<sub>id</sub>''.

If this equality does not hold, the participant MUST reject the key-generation result.

The participant MUST additionally establish that the accepted ''vss_commitment'' is the final VSS commitment associated with the secret share delivered to that participant. The key-generation procedure MUST provide participants with a consistent view of the same final VSS commitment. A participant MUST NOT accept a result if it cannot establish this association and consistency.

A participant MUST therefore reject the key-generation result if any of the following conditions holds:

* ''n'' or ''t'' does not satisfy the parameter requirements of this specification;
* ''id'' is outside the range ''0, ..., n - 1'';
* the VSS commitment does not contain exactly ''t'' elements;
* any commitment element fails the applicable extended-point decoding rules;
* ''A<sub>0</sub>'' is ''inf_point'';
* the secret share is not exactly 32 bytes;
* the secret share is zero or greater than or equal to ''q'';
* the Feldman verification equation does not hold;
* the VSS commitment is not the commitment authenticated as belonging to the received secret share; or
* the participant cannot establish the required consistent view of the final VSS commitment.

A participant MUST NOT use an unverified or rejected secret share as input to BIP 445 signing.

A verification failure MUST NOT be repaired by modifying the received secret share, participant identifier, evaluation point, commitment, or associated public key-generation data.

If any required participant rejects its key-generation result, the key-generation ceremony MUST NOT be declared successfully completed. An application MAY begin a new key-generation attempt, but this specification does not define replacement or repair of individual shares within a failed ceremony.

Secret shares accepted under different VSS commitments or different key-generation results MUST NOT be combined as if they belonged to a single threshold key.

=== Threshold Public Key Derivation ===

The threshold public key MUST be derived from the constant-term commitment of the final accepted VSS commitment.

Let the final VSS commitment be

:''(B<sub>0</sub>, B<sub>1</sub>, ..., B<sub>t-1</sub>)''.

The notation ''B<sub>j</sub>'' in this section refers to the commitment elements after all mandatory key-generation transformations defined by this specification have completed.

The threshold public point is

:''P = B<sub>0</sub>''.

The value ''B<sub>0</sub>'' MUST be a valid secp256k1 point other than ''inf_point''.

The serialized threshold public key is

:''thresh_pk = cbytes(B<sub>0</sub>)''.

Thus, ''thresh_pk'' is determined entirely by the final accepted VSS commitment. The dealer MUST NOT generate, select, or substitute an independent threshold public key unrelated to ''B<sub>0</sub>''.

A participant MUST derive ''thresh_pk'' from the final VSS commitment rather than relying solely on a separately supplied threshold public key.

If a threshold public key is supplied separately as part of the key-generation data, the participant MUST verify

:''cpoint(thresh_pk) = B<sub>0</sub>''

and equivalently

:''thresh_pk = cbytes(B<sub>0</sub>)''.

If this equality does not hold, the participant MUST reject the key-generation result. A separately supplied threshold public key MUST NOT override the value derived from the final VSS commitment.

If the final sharing polynomial is

:''f<sub>final</sub>(x) =
b<sub>0</sub> +
b<sub>1</sub>x +
... +
b<sub>t-1</sub>x<sup>t-1</sup>'',

then

:''B<sub>0</sub> = b<sub>0</sub>G''.

Accordingly, the threshold public key is the public point corresponding to the secret represented by the constant term of the final sharing polynomial.

For any valid signer set, interpolation of the corresponding participant public shares using the BIP 445 identifier-to-evaluation-point mapping MUST reconstruct the same threshold public point ''B<sub>0</sub>''.

The resulting ''thresh_pk'' is the plain threshold public key representation consumed by [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445]. It is represented as a 33-byte compressed secp256k1 public key.

The term '''plain''' describes the public-key representation and MUST NOT be interpreted to mean that the underlying point has not undergone a key-generation transformation. In particular, any Taproot-safety transformation required by this specification MUST already be reflected in ''B<sub>0</sub>'' before ''thresh_pk'' is derived.

The resulting ''thresh_pk'' is not itself a 32-byte x-only public key, a Taproot witness program, or a Taproot output key. Conversion to x-only form and subsequent signing- or application-level key tweaking are performed according to BIP 445 and the applicable Bitcoin specifications.

=== Participant Public Share Derivation ===

Participant public shares MUST be derived from the final accepted sharing represented by the final VSS commitment.

Let the final VSS commitment be

:''(B<sub>0</sub>, B<sub>1</sub>, ..., B<sub>t-1</sub>)''.

For every participant identifier

:''id ∈ {0, ..., n - 1}'',

define the corresponding Shamir evaluation point

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''.

The participant public point is obtained by evaluating the final VSS commitment at this evaluation point:

:''P<sub>id</sub> =
B<sub>0</sub> +
x<sub>id</sub>B<sub>1</sub> +
x<sub>id</sub><sup>2</sup>B<sub>2</sub> +
... +
x<sub>id</sub><sup>t-1</sup>B<sub>t-1</sub>''.

The point ''P<sub>id</sub>'' MUST NOT be ''inf_point''.

The corresponding BIP 445 participant public share is the ordinary compressed serialization

:''pubshare<sub>id</sub> = cbytes(P<sub>id</sub>)''.

Because the final Feldman commitment satisfies

:''B<sub>j</sub> = b<sub>j</sub>G''

for the coefficients of the final sharing polynomial

:''f<sub>final</sub>(x) =
b<sub>0</sub> +
b<sub>1</sub>x +
... +
b<sub>t-1</sub>x<sup>t-1</sup>'',

the public point may equivalently be written as

# :''P<sub>id</sub>

f<sub>final</sub>(x<sub>id</sub>)G''.

For the final participant secret share

:''secshare<sub>id</sub> =
f<sub>final</sub>(x<sub>id</sub>)'',

it therefore follows that

:''P<sub>id</sub> =
secshare<sub>id</sub>G''

and consequently

:''pubshare<sub>id</sub> =
cbytes(secshare<sub>id</sub>G)''.

A participant that possesses ''secshare<sub>id</sub>'' MUST verify that the public point derived directly from its secret share equals the public point derived from the final VSS commitment. Failure of this equality MUST cause rejection of the key-generation result.

Because the final VSS commitment is public, knowledge of a participant secret share is not required to derive participant public shares. Any party possessing the accepted final VSS commitment and the participant identifiers MAY derive the complete public-share list

:''pubshares =
[pubshare<sub>0</sub>,
pubshare<sub>1</sub>,
...,
pubshare<sub>n-1</sub>]''.

If a participant public share or complete public-share list is supplied separately, each supplied public share MUST decode as a valid ordinary compressed secp256k1 point and MUST equal the corresponding value derived from the final VSS commitment.

For every supplied ''pubshare<sub>id</sub>'',

:''cpoint(pubshare<sub>id</sub>) = P<sub>id</sub>''

MUST hold.

A separately supplied public share MUST NOT override the value derived from the final VSS commitment. If any supplied public share differs from its VSS-derived value, the associated public key-generation data MUST be rejected.

For any valid signer set ''S'' containing at least ''t'' distinct participants, let ''lambda<sub>i</sub>'' denote the BIP 445 Lagrange coefficient for participant ''i'', computed using the evaluation-point mapping ''x<sub>i</sub> = i + 1''. The participant public points MUST satisfy

:''B<sub>0</sub> =
Σ<sub>i ∈ S</sub>
lambda<sub>i</sub>P<sub>i</sub>''.

Accordingly,

:''cbytes(B<sub>0</sub>) = thresh_pk''

is the threshold public key reconstructed from any valid signer set according to BIP 445.

The participant public-share list produced by this specification is therefore directly usable when constructing a BIP 445 ''SignersContext''. A signing session selects the entries corresponding to its chosen signer identifiers; key generation does not alter or renumber those identifiers when public shares are selected for signing.

=== Completion ===

A key-generation ceremony completes successfully only after all mandatory key-generation transformations have completed, the final key-generation material has passed the dealer-side validation requirements of this specification, the final secret shares have been distributed to the designated participants, and the participants have successfully performed the acceptance procedure defined in [[#Participant Acceptance|Participant Acceptance]].

All participants in a successful ceremony MUST have a consistent view of the same final VSS commitment.

If any designated participant rejects its secret share, accepts a different VSS commitment, or otherwise fails the participant-acceptance procedure, the key-generation ceremony MUST NOT be declared successfully completed.

An application MAY begin a new key-generation attempt after a failure. It MUST NOT silently remove a failed participant, substitute a participant, change ''n'' or ''t'', or combine material from different attempts and treat the result as completion of the original ceremony.

Let the final accepted sharing polynomial be

:''f<sub>final</sub>(x) =
b<sub>0</sub> +
b<sub>1</sub>x +
... +
b<sub>t-1</sub>x<sup>t-1</sup> mod q''

and let the final accepted VSS commitment be

:''(B<sub>0</sub>, B<sub>1</sub>, ..., B<sub>t-1</sub>)''

where

:''B<sub>j</sub> = b<sub>j</sub>G''

for every

:''0 ≤ j < t''.

For a successful key-generation result, the following invariants MUST hold.

The final constant coefficient MUST be nonzero:

:''1 ≤ b<sub>0</sub> < q''.

The final constant-term commitment MUST therefore be a non-infinity point:

:''B<sub>0</sub> = b<sub>0</sub>G != inf_point''.

The threshold public key MUST be the ordinary compressed serialization of this point:

:''thresh_pk = cbytes(B<sub>0</sub>)''.

For every participant identifier

:''id ∈ {0, ..., n - 1}'',

define

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''.

The final participant secret share MUST satisfy

:''secshare<sub>id</sub> =
f<sub>final</sub>(x<sub>id</sub>) mod q''

and

:''1 ≤ secshare<sub>id</sub> < q''.

Define the participant public point

:''P<sub>id</sub> =
secshare<sub>id</sub>G''.

The point ''P<sub>id</sub>'' MUST be non-infinity and MUST also equal evaluation of the final VSS commitment at the participant's Shamir evaluation point:

:''P<sub>id</sub> =
B<sub>0</sub> +
x<sub>id</sub>B<sub>1</sub> +
x<sub>id</sub><sup>2</sup>B<sub>2</sub> +
... +
x<sub>id</sub><sup>t-1</sup>B<sub>t-1</sub>''.

The participant's BIP 445 public share MUST be

:''pubshare<sub>id</sub> =
cbytes(P<sub>id</sub>)''.

Accordingly, every successful participant output MUST satisfy

:''pubshare<sub>id</sub> =
cbytes(secshare<sub>id</sub>G)''.

For any valid subset ''S'' containing at least ''t'' distinct participants, interpolation of the corresponding participant public points using the identifier-to-evaluation-point mapping

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''

MUST reconstruct the same threshold public point:

:''B<sub>0</sub> =
Σ<sub>i ∈ S</sub>
lambda<sub>i</sub>P<sub>i</sub>'',

where ''lambda<sub>i</sub>'' is the corresponding Lagrange interpolation coefficient.

Thus, all participant shares, participant public shares, the final VSS commitment, and ''thresh_pk'' MUST represent one common final Shamir sharing and one common threshold public key.

Initial, intermediate, rejected, or superseded key-generation values MUST NOT be treated as outputs of a successfully completed ceremony.

Upon successful completion, the dealer MUST delete its retained secret key-generation state as specified in [[#Key-Generation State|Key-Generation State]]. Subsequent FROST signing MUST NOT depend on continued possession of the dealer's copy of the final threshold secret, polynomial coefficients, or participant secret shares.

The public key-generation state, including ''n'', ''t'', ''thresh_pk'', ''pubshares'', and ''vss_commitment'', MAY be retained and distributed. Implementations retaining these values MUST preserve their association with the key-generation result to which they belong.

=== Compatibility with BIP 445 ===

The final output of this key-generation procedure MUST satisfy the key-material requirements of [https://github.com/siv2r/bip-frost-signing BIP 445].

This specification uses the BIP 445 distinction between a '''participant''' and a '''signer'''. A participant is one of the ''n'' parties for whom key material was generated. A signer is a participant selected to take part in a particular signing session.

Key generation produces material for participant identifiers

:''id ∈ {0, ..., n - 1}''.

For each participant, the final key-generation output MUST satisfy

:''1 ≤ secshare<sub>id</sub> < q'',

:''P<sub>id</sub> = secshare<sub>id</sub>G'',

and

:''pubshare<sub>id</sub> = cbytes(P<sub>id</sub>)''.

The final threshold public key MUST be

:''thresh_pk = cbytes(B<sub>0</sub>)'',

where ''B<sub>0</sub>'' is the constant-term commitment of the final accepted VSS commitment.

Accordingly:

* each participant identifier is an integer in ''0, ..., n - 1'';
* each participant secret share is a canonical 32-byte nonzero secp256k1 scalar;
* each participant public share is a canonical 33-byte compressed non-infinity secp256k1 point;
* ''thresh_pk'' is a canonical 33-byte compressed non-infinity secp256k1 point; and
* participant public shares interpolate to the same threshold public point represented by ''thresh_pk''.

For a BIP 445 signing session, let ''u'' participants be selected as signers, where

:''t ≤ u ≤ n''.

The BIP 445 ''SignersContext'' is constructed using the identifiers and public shares corresponding to those selected participants together with the common ''n'', ''t'', and ''thresh_pk'' values produced by key generation.

This specification maps participant identifier ''id'' to the Shamir evaluation point

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''.

This mapping is identical to the interpolation convention used by BIP 445.

For any valid signer set ''S'' containing at least ''t'' distinct participants, the selected public points MUST satisfy

:''B<sub>0</sub> =
Σ<sub>i ∈ S</sub>
lambda<sub>i</sub>P<sub>i</sub>'',

where ''lambda<sub>i</sub>'' is the BIP 445 Lagrange coefficient computed using the participant evaluation points ''id + 1''.

A key-generation implementation MUST NOT renumber participants or substitute a different identifier-to-evaluation-point mapping when constructing BIP 445 key material.

Long-term key-generation material MUST NOT be modified before use with BIP 445 except by a transformation explicitly defined by this specification or by the applicable BIP 445 algorithms.

The ''vss_commitment'' is not an input to a BIP 445 signing session. It is key-generation data used to establish and verify the relationships among the participant secret shares, participant public shares, and threshold public key.

BIP 445's ''ValidateSignersCtx'' algorithm verifies that the supplied signing context is functionally consistent, including that the selected participant public shares interpolate to ''thresh_pk''. Successful ''ValidateSignersCtx'' validation MUST NOT be treated as a substitute for the key-generation verification and security requirements defined by this specification.

=== Taproot Safety and Use ===

The final threshold public key produced by this specification MUST satisfy the Taproot-safety requirement imposed by BIP 445.

BIP 445 requires key-generation protocols to output a threshold public key that is committed to an unspendable Taproot script path as recommended by BIP 341. This prevents key-generation output from being used as a threshold public key while containing an independently chosen hidden Taproot script-path commitment.

This requirement is implemented as a mandatory key-generation transformation of the initial Shamir sharing and its Feldman commitment.

==== Taproot-Safety Transformation ====

Let the initial sharing polynomial be

:''f(x) =
a<sub>0</sub> +
a<sub>1</sub>x +
... +
a<sub>t-1</sub>x<sup>t-1</sup>''

with initial Feldman commitments

:''A<sub>j</sub> = a<sub>j</sub>G''.

The initial threshold public point is

:''A<sub>0</sub> = a<sub>0</sub>G''.

Let

:''p = xbytes(A<sub>0</sub>)''.

Define the parity scalar ''g'' by

:''g = 1''

if ''A<sub>0</sub>'' has an even Y coordinate, and

:''g = -1 mod q''

otherwise.

Thus,

:''gA<sub>0</sub> = with_even_y(A<sub>0</sub>)''.

The dealer computes the BIP 341 TapTweak hash for an unspendable script path:

:''h = hash<sub>TapTweak</sub>(p)''.

Let

:''tau = int(h)'',

where ''int'' interprets the 32-byte hash as an unsigned big-endian integer.

If

:''tau >= q'',

the Taproot-safety transformation MUST fail.

The final polynomial coefficients are defined as

:''b<sub>0</sub> =
g a<sub>0</sub> + tau mod q''

and, for every ''j > 0'',

:''b<sub>j</sub> =
g a<sub>j</sub> mod q''.

Equivalently, the final sharing polynomial is

:''f<sub>final</sub>(x) =
g f(x) + tau mod q''.

The corresponding final VSS commitment is

:''B<sub>0</sub> =
gA<sub>0</sub> + tau G''

and, for every ''j > 0'',

:''B<sub>j</sub> =
gA<sub>j</sub>''.

The final constant-term commitment therefore satisfies

:''B<sub>0</sub> =
with_even_y(A<sub>0</sub>) + tau G''.

This is the BIP 341 no-script-path Taproot construction applied to the initial threshold public point.

The point ''B<sub>0</sub>'' MUST NOT be ''inf_point''. If it is ''inf_point'', the key-generation attempt MUST fail.

For each participant identifier ''id'', let the initial share be

:''d<sub>id</sub> =
f(id + 1)''.

The corresponding final participant secret share is

:''secshare<sub>id</sub> =
g d<sub>id</sub> + tau mod q''.

Every resulting final secret share MUST satisfy

:''1 ≤ secshare<sub>id</sub> < q''.

If any final secret share is zero, the current sharing MUST NOT be accepted as BIP 445 key material and the key-generation procedure MUST follow the applicable failure or retry rules.

The corresponding final participant public point is

:''P<sub>id</sub> =
secshare<sub>id</sub>G''

and MUST also equal evaluation of the final VSS commitment:

:''P<sub>id</sub> =
B<sub>0</sub> +
(id + 1)B<sub>1</sub> +
... +
(id + 1)<sup>t-1</sup>B<sub>t-1</sub>''.

After successful application of the Taproot-safety transformation, the final VSS commitment is

:''vss_commitment =
(B<sub>0</sub>, B<sub>1</sub>, ..., B<sub>t-1</sub>)''

and the final threshold public key is

:''thresh_pk = cbytes(B<sub>0</sub>)''.

The Taproot-safety transformation is part of key generation. The resulting participant secret shares, participant public shares, VSS commitment, and threshold public key are the long-term key-generation outputs consumed by BIP 445.

The term '''plain threshold public key''' in BIP 445 refers to the 33-byte compressed representation of ''thresh_pk''. It does not imply that the underlying threshold public point has not undergone the Taproot-safety transformation defined above.

==== Application-Level Taproot Tweaking ====

The Taproot-safety transformation performed during key generation MUST NOT be confused with a subsequent Taproot tweak used to construct an actual Bitcoin Taproot output.

After key generation, an application MAY use the resulting ''thresh_pk'' as the basis for a Taproot internal key. Application-level tweaking and FROST signing for the resulting tweaked threshold key MUST be performed using the BIP 445 tweaking algorithms and the rules of BIP 341.

For an application-level BIP 341 X-only tweak, BIP 445 initializes a tweak context from the final ''thresh_pk'' and applies the tweak in X-only mode. Such application-level tweaks are evaluated during signing and do not modify the long-term participant secret shares produced by this key-generation procedure.

If a Taproot output has no spendable script path, the application follows the BIP 341 no-script-path output-key construction using the final threshold public key as its internal key.

If a Taproot output contains a script tree, the application MUST include the applicable Taproot Merkle root when deriving the BIP 341 TapTweak.

The 32-byte x-only serialization of a non-infinity threshold point ''P'' is

:''xbytes(P) = cbytes(P)[1:33]''.

Discarding the compressed-key prefix therefore produces the correct x-only serialization, but discards the Y-parity information of the original full point. X-only point interpretation and any required parity normalization MUST follow BIP 340, BIP 341, and BIP 445.

This specification does not define Taproot script trees, Taproot control blocks, transaction output construction, or Bitcoin transaction validation.

== Algorithms ==

The algorithms in this section define the normative computational behavior of the trusted-dealer key-generation procedure.

An algorithm specified to '''fail''' MUST NOT return usable key-generation material from that invocation.

Unless otherwise specified, all scalar arithmetic is performed modulo the secp256k1 group order ''q''.

=== Notation ===

The following notation is used:

* ''Scalar'' denotes an element of the secp256k1 scalar field;
* ''Point'' denotes an element of the secp256k1 group;
* ''G'' denotes the secp256k1 generator;
* ''q'' denotes the order of ''G'';
* ''inf_point'' denotes the point at infinity;
* ''Scalar(x)'' converts an integer ''x'' satisfying ''0 <= x < q'' to a scalar;
* ''scalar_to_bytes(s)'' returns the canonical 32-byte big-endian encoding of scalar ''s'';
* ''scalar_from_bytes_checked(b)'' parses a canonical 32-byte scalar in the range ''0 <= s < q'' and fails otherwise;
* ''scalar_from_bytes_nonzero_checked(b)'' performs the same operation but additionally fails if ''s = 0'';
* ''cbytes(P)'' returns the 33-byte compressed encoding of a non-infinity secp256k1 point;
* ''cpoint(b)'' parses such an encoding and fails if the input is invalid or represents no non-infinity secp256k1 point;
* ''cbytes_ext(P)'' equals ''cbytes(P)'' for a non-infinity point and equals 33 zero bytes for ''inf_point'';
* ''cpoint_ext(b)'' parses the corresponding extended encoding;
* ''xbytes(P)'' denotes the 32-byte X-coordinate serialization of a non-infinity point; and
* ''hash<sub>TapTweak</sub>(m)'' denotes the BIP341 tagged hash with tag ''TapTweak''.

Where two values have already been parsed as group elements, algorithms SHOULD compare the group elements directly rather than comparing serializations.

=== Polynomial Evaluation ===

The algorithm

:''PolynomialEvaluate(coeffs, x)''

evaluates a scalar polynomial using Horner's method.

Inputs:

* ''coeffs'', a non-empty coefficient list in ascending degree order; and
* ''x'', a scalar.

Algorithm:

* Let ''value = Scalar(0)''.
* For each ''coeff'' in ''reverse(coeffs)'':
  ** Let ''value = value * x + coeff''.
* Return ''value''.

For

:''coeffs =
[a<sub>0</sub>, a<sub>1</sub>, ..., a<sub>t-1</sub>]''

the returned value is

:''a<sub>0</sub> +
a<sub>1</sub>x +
... +
a<sub>t-1</sub>x<sup>t-1</sup> mod q''.

=== Polynomial Evaluation in the Group ===

The algorithm

:''PolynomialEvaluatePoint(commitments, x)''

evaluates a committed polynomial using Horner's method in the secp256k1 group.

Inputs:

* ''commitments'', a non-empty ordered list of group elements; and
* ''x'', a scalar.

Algorithm:

* Let ''result = inf_point''.
* For each ''A'' in ''reverse(commitments)'':
  ** Let ''result = x * result + A''.
* Return ''result''.

If

:''commitments[j] = coeffs[j]G''

for every ''j'', then

# :''PolynomialEvaluatePoint(commitments, x)

PolynomialEvaluate(coeffs, x)G''.

Commitment elements supplied to this algorithm MAY include ''inf_point''.

=== Participant Evaluation Point ===

The algorithm

:''ParticipantEvaluationPoint(id, n)''

returns the Shamir evaluation point associated with participant ''id''.

Algorithm:

* Fail unless ''1 <= n < 2<sup>32</sup>''.
* Fail unless ''0 <= id < n''.
* Let ''x = id + 1''.
* Return ''Scalar(x)''.

Because

:''1 <= id + 1 <= n < 2<sup>32</sup> < q'',

the conversion always succeeds for a valid participant identifier.

The value ''id + 1'' is a Shamir evaluation point only. The participant's protocol identifier remains ''id''.

=== Parsing a VSS Commitment ===

The algorithm

:''ParseVSSCommitment(vss_commitment, t)''

parses a serialized final VSS commitment.

Algorithm:

* Fail if ''t < 1''.
* Fail unless
  :''len(vss_commitment) = 33t''.
* Let ''commitments = []''.
* For ''j = 0 .. t - 1'':
  ** Let
  :''enc = vss_commitment[33j : 33(j+1)]''.
  ** Let
  :''B<sub>j</sub> = cpoint_ext(enc)''.
  ** Fail if parsing fails.
  ** If ''j = 0'', fail if
  :''B<sub>0</sub> = inf_point''.
  ** Append ''B<sub>j</sub>'' to ''commitments''.
* Return ''commitments''.

A non-constant commitment

:''B<sub>j</sub> = inf_point''

for ''j > 0'' is valid and represents a zero polynomial coefficient.

The constant-term commitment MUST be non-infinity.

=== Taproot-Safety Tweak ===

The algorithm

:''TaprootSafetyTweak(P)''

derives the mandatory key-generation tweak used to make the resulting threshold public key Taproot-safe as required by BIP 445.

Input:

* ''P'', the initial non-infinity threshold public point.

Algorithm:

* Fail if
  :''P = inf_point''.
* Let
  :''h = hash<sub>TapTweak</sub>(xbytes(P))''.
* Interpret ''h'' as an unsigned 256-bit big-endian integer ''v''.
* Fail if
  :''v >= q''.
* Return
  :''tau = Scalar(v)''.

This algorithm uses the BIP341 X-only serialization of ''P'' as the TapTweak hash input.

The key-generation transformation defined here is an additive transformation of the Shamir sharing. It MUST NOT perform the X-only parity normalization used by BIP 445's signing-time ''ApplyXonlyTweak'' algorithm.

=== Applying the Taproot-Safety Transformation ===

The algorithm

:''ApplyTaprootSafety(coeffs, commitments)''

transforms the initial sharing into the final BIP 445 key-generation sharing.

Inputs:

* ''coeffs =
  [a<sub>0</sub>, ..., a<sub>t-1</sub>]''; and
* ''commitments =
  [A<sub>0</sub>, ..., A<sub>t-1</sub>]''

with

:''A<sub>j</sub> = a<sub>j</sub>G''.

Algorithm:

* Fail unless
  :''len(coeffs) = len(commitments) >= 1''.
* Fail if
  :''A<sub>0</sub> = inf_point''.
* Let
  :''tau = TaprootSafetyTweak(A<sub>0</sub>)''; fail if that fails.
* Let
  :''b<sub>0</sub> =
  a<sub>0</sub> + tau mod q''.
* Fail if
  :''b<sub>0</sub> = 0''.
* For ''j = 1 .. t - 1'', let
  :''b<sub>j</sub> = a<sub>j</sub>''.
* Let
  :''B<sub>0</sub> =
  A<sub>0</sub> + tau G''.
* Fail if
  :''B<sub>0</sub> = inf_point''.
* For ''j = 1 .. t - 1'', let
  :''B<sub>j</sub> = A<sub>j</sub>''.
* Fail if
  :''B<sub>0</sub> != b<sub>0</sub>G''.
* Return
  :''(final_coeffs, final_commitments, tau)''
  where
  :''final_coeffs =
  [b<sub>0</sub>, ..., b<sub>t-1</sub>]''
  and
  :''final_commitments =
  [B<sub>0</sub>, ..., B<sub>t-1</sub>]''.

Equivalently,

# :''f<sub>final</sub>(x)

f(x) + tau mod q''.

Thus every participant share is shifted by the same scalar ''tau''.

=== Deriving a Public Share from a VSS Commitment ===

The algorithm

:''DerivePubshareFromCommitment(id, n, t, vss_commitment)''

derives a participant public share from the final VSS commitment.

Algorithm:

* Let
  :''x = ParticipantEvaluationPoint(id, n)'';
  fail if that fails.
* Let
  :''commitments =
  ParseVSSCommitment(vss_commitment, t)'';
  fail if that fails.
* Let
  :''P =
  PolynomialEvaluatePoint(commitments, x)''.
* Fail if
  :''P = inf_point''.
* Return
  :''cbytes(P)''.

=== Deriving the Threshold Public Key from a VSS Commitment ===

The algorithm

:''ThreshPubkeyFromCommitment(t, vss_commitment)''

derives the final threshold public key.

Algorithm:

* Let
  :''commitments =
  ParseVSSCommitment(vss_commitment, t)'';
  fail if that fails.
* Let
  :''B<sub>0</sub> = commitments[0]''.
* Return
  :''cbytes(B<sub>0</sub>)''.

Because ''ParseVSSCommitment'' rejects an infinity constant-term commitment, no additional infinity check is necessary.

=== Secret-Share Verification ===

The algorithm

:''VerifySecshare(id, n, t, secshare, vss_commitment)''

verifies a final participant secret share.

Algorithm:

* Let
  :''d =
  scalar_from_bytes_nonzero_checked(secshare)''.
* Return ''False'' if parsing fails.
* Let
  :''x =
  ParticipantEvaluationPoint(id, n)''.
* Return ''False'' if that fails.
* Let
  :''commitments =
  ParseVSSCommitment(vss_commitment, t)''.
* Return ''False'' if that fails.
* Let
  :''P<sub>expected</sub> =
  PolynomialEvaluatePoint(commitments, x)''.
* Let
  :''P<sub>actual</sub> = dG''.
* Return
  :''P<sub>actual</sub> = P<sub>expected</sub>''.

The algorithm compares group elements directly. It does not attempt to serialize ''P<sub>expected</sub>'', which may be ''inf_point'' for malformed or inconsistent public input.

Because ''d'' is required to be nonzero,

:''P<sub>actual</sub> != inf_point''.

Therefore an infinity ''P<sub>expected</sub>'' necessarily causes verification failure.

An invalid secret-share encoding MUST NOT be reduced modulo ''q'' or otherwise transformed into a valid scalar.

=== Trusted Dealer Key Generation ===

The algorithm

:''TrustedDealerKeygen(n, t, seckey)''

performs randomized trusted-dealer key generation.

Inputs:

* ''n'', the number of participants;
* ''t'', the configured threshold; and
* optional ''seckey'', a 32-byte canonical encoding of a nonzero secp256k1 scalar.

Algorithm:

* Fail unless
  :''1 <= t <= n < 2<sup>32</sup>''.

If ''seckey'' is present:

* Fail unless
  :''len(seckey) = 32''.
* Let
  :''a<sub>0</sub> =
  scalar_from_bytes_nonzero_checked(seckey)''.
* Fail if parsing fails.

Otherwise:

* Uniformly sample
  :''a<sub>0</sub> ∈ {1, ..., q - 1}''
  using a cryptographically secure random-scalar procedure.

Generate the non-constant coefficients independently:

* For ''j = 1 .. t - 1'':
  ** Uniformly sample
  :''a<sub>j</sub> ∈ {0, ..., q - 1}''.

Let

:''coeffs =
[a<sub>0</sub>, ..., a<sub>t-1</sub>]''.

Invoke

:''TrustedDealerKeygenInternal(coeffs, n)''.

If that invocation fails because a final participant secret share is zero and ''t > 1'', the dealer MAY discard the non-constant coefficients, independently resample

:''a<sub>1</sub>, ..., a<sub>t-1</sub>'',

and retry while retaining ''a<sub>0</sub>''.

If an imported ''seckey'' was supplied, the dealer MUST NOT modify ''a<sub>0</sub>'' in order to repair a failed attempt.

Any other failure causes the current key-generation operation to fail.

No output from a failed attempt may be distributed or treated as key material.

=== Internal Trusted Dealer Key Generation ===

The deterministic algorithm

:''TrustedDealerKeygenInternal(coeffs, n)''

constructs final key-generation material from explicitly supplied polynomial coefficients.

Inputs:

* ''coeffs'', a non-empty coefficient list in ascending degree order; and
* ''n'', the number of participants.

Algorithm:

* Let
  :''t = len(coeffs)''.
* Fail unless
  :''1 <= t <= n < 2<sup>32</sup>''.
* Fail if
  :''coeffs[0] = Scalar(0)''.

Construct the initial Feldman commitments:

* For ''j = 0 .. t - 1'':
  ** Let
  :''A<sub>j</sub> = coeffs[j]G''.

Fail if

:''A<sub>0</sub> = inf_point''.

An initial non-constant commitment MAY be ''inf_point''.

Apply the mandatory key-generation safety transformation:

* Let
  :''(final_coeffs, final_commitments, tau) =
  ApplyTaprootSafety(coeffs, commitments)''.
* Fail if that fails.

Let

:''final_commitments =
[B<sub>0</sub>, ..., B<sub>t-1</sub>]''.

Serialize:

:''vss_commitment =
cbytes_ext(B<sub>0</sub>) ||
... ||
cbytes_ext(B<sub>t-1</sub>)''.

Because

:''B<sub>0</sub> != inf_point'',

the first element's extended encoding is identical to its ordinary compressed encoding.

Let

:''thresh_pk =
cbytes(B<sub>0</sub>)''.

Initialize:

:''secshares = []''

and:

:''pubshares = []''.

For each participant identifier

:''id = 0 .. n - 1'':

* Let
  :''x =
  ParticipantEvaluationPoint(id, n)''.
* Let
  :''d =
  PolynomialEvaluate(final_coeffs, x)''.
* Fail if
  :''d = Scalar(0)''.
* Let
  :''P = dG''.
* Fail if
  :''P = inf_point''.
* Let
  :''P<sub>expected</sub> =
  PolynomialEvaluatePoint(final_commitments, x)''.
* Fail unless
  :''P = P<sub>expected</sub>''.
* Let
  :''secshare =
  scalar_to_bytes(d)''.
* Let
  :''pubshare =
  cbytes(P)''.
* Append ''secshare'' to ''secshares''.
* Append ''pubshare'' to ''pubshares''.

After all participants have been processed, verify:

# :''ThreshPubkeyFromCommitment(t, vss_commitment)

thresh_pk''.

For every participant ''id'', verify:

:''VerifySecshare(
id,
n,
t,
secshares[id],
vss_commitment
) = True''.

Return

:''(vss_commitment,
thresh_pk,
pubshares,
secshares)''.

The lists ''pubshares'' and ''secshares'' are in participant-identifier order. Thus

:''pubshares[id]''

and

:''secshares[id]''

belong to participant ''id''.

=== Deterministic Key Generation from Explicit Coefficients ===

For implementation testing and test-vector generation, an implementation SHOULD provide an operation equivalent to

:''TrustedDealerKeygenInternal(coeffs, n)''.

This operation MUST NOT sample randomness.

Given identical ''coeffs'' and ''n'', it MUST either fail deterministically or produce identical:

* ''vss_commitment'';
* ''thresh_pk'';
* ''pubshares''; and
* ''secshares''.

This deterministic operation is intended for testing and interoperability verification. Production key generation MUST NOT use fixed test coefficients in place of secure random coefficient generation.

=== Participant Key Acceptance ===

The algorithm

:''ParticipantKeyAccept(
id,
n,
t,
secshare,
vss_commitment,
thresh_pk,
pubshares
)''

validates final key-generation material before a participant uses its secret share.

The ''thresh_pk'' and ''pubshares'' arguments MAY be omitted if the application derives those values exclusively from ''vss_commitment''.

Algorithm:

* Fail unless
  :''1 <= t <= n < 2<sup>32</sup>''.
* Fail unless
  :''0 <= id < n''.
* Let
  :''commitments =
  ParseVSSCommitment(vss_commitment, t)''.
* Fail if parsing fails.
* Fail unless
  :''VerifySecshare(
  id,
  n,
  t,
  secshare,
  vss_commitment
  ) = True''.
* Let
  :''derived_thresh_pk =
  ThreshPubkeyFromCommitment(
  t,
  vss_commitment
  )''.
* Fail if derivation fails.
* Let
  :''derived_pubshare =
  DerivePubshareFromCommitment(
  id,
  n,
  t,
  vss_commitment
  )''.
* Fail if derivation fails.

If ''thresh_pk'' is supplied:

* Parse it using ''cpoint''; fail if parsing fails.
* Fail unless
  :''thresh_pk = derived_thresh_pk''.

If ''pubshares'' is supplied:

* Fail unless
  :''len(pubshares) = n''.
* For every participant identifier
  :''j = 0 .. n - 1'':
  ** Let
  :''derived =
  DerivePubshareFromCommitment(
  j,
  n,
  t,
  vss_commitment
  )''.
  ** Fail if derivation fails.
  ** Parse ''pubshares[j]'' using ''cpoint''; fail if parsing fails.
  ** Fail unless
  :''pubshares[j] = derived''.

The participant MUST additionally establish, through the distribution mechanism defined elsewhere in this specification, that the accepted ''vss_commitment'' is the final commitment belonging to the current key-generation ceremony and that participants have the required consistent view of that commitment.

If all checks succeed, return the participant's accepted key material:

:''private =
(id, secshare)''

and public key-generation material:

:''public =
(n, t, derived_thresh_pk, pubshares, vss_commitment)''.

If the complete ''pubshares'' list was not supplied, it MAY be deterministically derived from ''vss_commitment''.

A participant MUST NOT use ''secshare'' for BIP 445 signing unless this acceptance procedure succeeds.

=== Failure Handling ===

A failed algorithm invocation MUST NOT expose partially generated output as usable key material.

An implementation MUST reject the current key-generation result if:

* the key-generation parameters are invalid;
* an imported threshold-secret input is invalid;
* the mandatory Taproot-safety transformation fails;
* the final threshold secret is zero;
* any final participant secret share is zero;
* the final threshold public point is infinity;
* a participant public point is infinity;
* a VSS commitment fails its encoding or parsing requirements;
* dealer-side scalar/group consistency checks fail; or
* a required participant fails the key-acceptance procedure.

An implementation MUST NOT repair failure by independently modifying one participant's secret share, public share, identifier, or VSS commitment.

A new attempt MUST produce one internally consistent set of shares and public data. Material from different failed or successful attempts MUST NOT be combined.

== Correctness ==

This section shows that the final secret shares, participant public shares, VSS commitment, and threshold public key produced by this specification describe one consistent Shamir sharing.

The arguments below establish functional correctness. They do not establish the security of the trusted dealer, randomness generation, secret-share transport, or secret erasure; those properties are addressed separately in [[#Security Considerations|Security Considerations]].

=== Final Sharing Polynomial ===

Let the final sharing polynomial be

:''f<sub>final</sub>(x) =
b<sub>0</sub> +
b<sub>1</sub>x +
... +
b<sub>t-1</sub>x<sup>t-1</sup> mod q''.

Its final VSS commitment is

:''B<sub>j</sub> = b<sub>j</sub>G''

for

:''0 <= j < t''.

For participant ''id'', define

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''

and

:''secshare<sub>id</sub> =
f<sub>final</sub>(x<sub>id</sub>)''.

The corresponding participant public point is

:''P<sub>id</sub> =
secshare<sub>id</sub>G''.

By linearity of scalar multiplication,

# :''P<sub>id</sub>

f<sub>final</sub>(x<sub>id</sub>)G''

:''=
(b<sub>0</sub> +
b<sub>1</sub>x<sub>id</sub> +
... +
b<sub>t-1</sub>x<sub>id</sub><sup>t-1</sup>)G''

:''=
B<sub>0</sub> +
x<sub>id</sub>B<sub>1</sub> +
... +
x<sub>id</sub><sup>t-1</sup>B<sub>t-1</sub>''.

Therefore

:''secshare<sub>id</sub>G =
PolynomialEvaluatePoint(
final_commitments,
x<sub>id</sub>)''.

This is exactly the equality checked by ''VerifySecshare''.

Consequently, every correctly generated final secret share verifies against the final VSS commitment.

=== Correctness of the Taproot-Safety Transformation ===

Before the mandatory Taproot-safety transformation, let

:''f(x) =
a<sub>0</sub> +
a<sub>1</sub>x +
... +
a<sub>t-1</sub>x<sup>t-1</sup>''

with commitments

:''A<sub>j</sub> = a<sub>j</sub>G''.

Let ''tau'' be the Taproot-safety scalar defined by [[#Taproot-Safety Tweak|Taproot-Safety Tweak]].

The transformation defines

:''f<sub>final</sub>(x) =
f(x) + tau mod q''.

Thus

:''b<sub>0</sub> =
a<sub>0</sub> + tau mod q''

and

:''b<sub>j</sub> =
a<sub>j</sub>''

for every ''j > 0''.

The final commitments are therefore

:''B<sub>0</sub> =
A<sub>0</sub> + tau G''

and

:''B<sub>j</sub> =
A<sub>j</sub>''

for every ''j > 0''.

If participant ''id'' initially has

:''d<sub>id</sub> =
f(x<sub>id</sub>)'',

then its final share is

:''secshare<sub>id</sub> =
d<sub>id</sub> + tau mod q''.

Therefore

# :''secshare<sub>id</sub>G

d<sub>id</sub>G + tau G''

and

:''=
A<sub>0</sub> + tau G
+
x<sub>id</sub>A<sub>1</sub>
+
... +
x<sub>id</sub><sup>t-1</sup>A<sub>t-1</sub>''

:''=
B<sub>0</sub>
+
x<sub>id</sub>B<sub>1</sub>
+
... +
x<sub>id</sub><sup>t-1</sup>B<sub>t-1</sub>''.

The Taproot-safety transformation therefore preserves the Feldman verification relationship while changing the shared constant term from ''a<sub>0</sub>'' to ''b<sub>0</sub>''.

=== Threshold-Key Reconstruction ===

Let ''S'' be any set of at least ''t'' distinct participant identifiers.

Because

:''x<sub>id</sub> = id + 1''

and

:''0 <= id < n < 2<sup>32</sup> < q'',

the evaluation points belonging to distinct participants are distinct nonzero scalars.

For each participant ''i'' in ''S'', define the Lagrange coefficient for evaluation at zero as

:''lambda<sub>i</sub> =
PRODUCT<sub>j in S, j != i</sub>
x<sub>j</sub> /
(x<sub>j</sub> - x<sub>i</sub>) mod q''.

Since

:''x<sub>j</sub> = id<sub>j</sub> + 1'',

this is equivalently the zero-based identifier form used by BIP 445:

:''lambda<sub>i</sub> =
PRODUCT<sub>j in S, j != i</sub>
(id<sub>j</sub> + 1) /
(id<sub>j</sub> - id<sub>i</sub>) mod q''.

Lagrange interpolation at zero gives

:''b<sub>0</sub> =
SUM<sub>i in S</sub>
lambda<sub>i</sub>
secshare<sub>i</sub> mod q''.

Multiplying both sides by ''G'' gives

:''B<sub>0</sub> =
SUM<sub>i in S</sub>
lambda<sub>i</sub>P<sub>i</sub>''.

Because

:''pubshare<sub>i</sub> = cbytes(P<sub>i</sub>)''

and

:''thresh_pk = cbytes(B<sub>0</sub>)'',

the participant public shares supplied to BIP 445 reconstruct exactly the threshold public point represented by ''thresh_pk''.

This is the relationship checked by BIP 445 when validating a Signers Context.

=== Threshold One ===

The case ''t = 1'' is a valid degenerate threshold configuration.

The final polynomial is constant:

:''f<sub>final</sub>(x) = b<sub>0</sub>''.

Consequently every participant receives

:''secshare<sub>id</sub> = b<sub>0</sub>''

and every participant public point equals

:''P<sub>id</sub> = B<sub>0</sub>''.

Thus the construction remains algebraically correct, although a ''1-of-n'' configuration provides no threshold secrecy because any single participant possesses the complete signing secret.

== Security Considerations ==

This specification describes trusted-dealer key generation. Its security therefore depends on assumptions that are substantially stronger than those of a distributed key-generation protocol.

=== Trusted Dealer ===

During key generation, the dealer learns:

* the complete initial threshold secret;
* all polynomial coefficients;
* every participant secret share; and
* all intermediate secret values used to construct the final sharing.

A malicious or compromised dealer can therefore compromise the threshold key regardless of the configured threshold ''t''.

In particular, Feldman verification does not prove that the dealer used unbiased randomness, kept secret values confidential, distributed shares only to authorized participants, or erased its copies after key generation.

A deployment in which no single party is permitted to learn the complete threshold secret MUST use an appropriate distributed key-generation protocol instead of this specification.

The trusted dealer is required only for key generation. Successful later BIP 445 signing MUST NOT depend on continued participation by the dealer.

=== Randomness and Imported Secrets ===

New threshold secrets and non-constant polynomial coefficients MUST be sampled using cryptographically secure randomness as specified by this document.

Weak or predictable dealer randomness can reduce or completely eliminate the security of the resulting threshold key while all Feldman verification checks still succeed.

When an existing secret is imported, this procedure does not increase the entropy or restore the confidentiality of that secret.

The mandatory Taproot-safety tweak is public and deterministic. Consequently, if an imported secret is known or guessable, applying the Taproot-safety transformation does not make the resulting threshold secret secure.

Invalid imported secret encodings MUST be rejected rather than repaired through reduction, hashing, clamping, truncation, or another implicit transformation.

=== Feldman Commitments ===

The VSS commitment allows participants to verify that their shares are evaluations of one committed polynomial.

For coefficients

:''b<sub>j</sub>''

the commitments are

:''B<sub>j</sub> = b<sub>j</sub>G''.

These commitments are binding under the discrete-logarithm assumption for secp256k1, but they are not information-theoretically hiding.

Assuming the discrete logarithm problem is infeasible, publishing the commitments does not reveal their scalar coefficients. The VSS commitment does reveal public structural information, including the threshold ''t''.

Successful verification proves share consistency with the accepted commitment. It does not prove that the dealer behaved honestly in any respect not captured by that algebraic relationship.

=== Share Confidentiality and Compromise ===

A participant secret share is long-term private signing-key material and MUST be protected accordingly.

Secret-share transport MUST provide the confidentiality, integrity, authentication, and key-generation-context binding required by [[#Secret-Share Distribution|Secret-Share Distribution]].

Possession of fewer than ''t'' valid participant shares is insufficient to reconstruct the threshold secret through Shamir interpolation.

Possession of ''t'' or more distinct valid shares permits reconstruction of the threshold secret and therefore compromises the threshold key.

The case ''t = 1'' provides no threshold secrecy: every participant share is sufficient to recover the signing secret.

Loss of more than ''n - t'' participant shares prevents formation of a threshold signing set unless some recovery mechanism outside this specification is available.

=== Commitment Consistency and Equivocation ===

All participants in one successful key-generation ceremony MUST accept the same final ''vss_commitment''.

A malicious dealer that can present different commitments to different participants could otherwise create multiple individually valid but mutually incompatible sharings.

Authenticated point-to-point delivery alone does not establish that all participants received the same commitment.

The application MUST therefore provide the commitment-consistency mechanism required by [[#Secret-Share Distribution|Secret-Share Distribution]] and [[#Participant Acceptance|Participant Acceptance]].

Shares or public key material associated with different VSS commitments or different key-generation results MUST NOT be combined.

=== Secret Erasure ===

After successful key generation and distribution, the dealer no longer needs the threshold secret, polynomial coefficients, participant secret shares, or temporary secret intermediates.

The dealer MUST logically delete such secret state as required by [[#Key-Generation State|Key-Generation State]].

Implementations SHOULD minimize the lifetime and number of copies of secret values and SHOULD use platform-appropriate secure-memory and zeroization facilities where available.

This specification cannot guarantee that high-level runtimes, operating systems, storage systems, swap, crash dumps, compiler optimizations, or hardware have physically removed every residual copy of secret material.

Dealer erasure reduces the period during which compromise of the dealer compromises the threshold key; it does not undo disclosure that occurred before erasure.

=== Side Channels ===

Operations involving secret scalars SHOULD use implementations designed to resist timing and other relevant side-channel attacks.

Production implementations SHOULD use mature secp256k1 implementations and SHOULD avoid secret-dependent control flow or memory access where practical.

The pseudocode in this specification defines functional behavior only and MUST NOT be interpreted as demonstrating constant-time execution, fault resistance, secure memory handling, or resistance to physical attacks.

=== Taproot Safety ===

The mandatory Taproot-safety transformation defined by this specification protects against a key-generation party choosing threshold key material that carries a hidden spendable Taproot script path capable of bypassing the intended threshold policy.

This protection does not make an otherwise malicious trusted dealer safe. A malicious dealer already knows the threshold secret during key generation and can compromise the key directly.

Application-level Taproot tweaks performed after key generation are separate from the mandatory key-generation Taproot-safety transformation and MUST follow BIP 341 and BIP 445.

=== BIP 445 Validation ===

BIP 445's ''ValidateSignersCtx'' verifies functional consistency of signing key material, including whether the selected participant public shares reconstruct the supplied threshold public key.

Successful BIP 445 validation does not establish that this trusted-dealer procedure was executed securely.

In particular, it does not establish secure randomness, confidential share delivery, consistent VSS distribution, dealer erasure, or absence of secret leakage.

Implementations MUST therefore perform the key-generation validation required by this specification rather than relying on BIP 445 signing-context validation as a substitute.

== Test Vectors ==

Test vectors accompanying this specification SHOULD permit independent verification of the complete deterministic portion of trusted-dealer key generation.

All scalar and point values MUST use the encodings defined in [[#Encodings|Encodings]]. Hexadecimal byte strings are written in big-endian order unless otherwise specified.

Test vectors generated from explicitly supplied polynomial coefficients are deterministic. This does not define deterministic production key generation.

=== Vector Contents ===

A complete successful test vector SHOULD contain:

* ''n'' and ''t'';
* the initial polynomial coefficients
  ''a<sub>0</sub>, ..., a<sub>t-1</sub>'';
* the initial commitments
  ''A<sub>0</sub>, ..., A<sub>t-1</sub>'';
* the Taproot-safety scalar ''tau'';
* the final polynomial coefficients
  ''b<sub>0</sub>, ..., b<sub>t-1</sub>'';
* the final serialized ''vss_commitment'';
* ''thresh_pk'';
* every participant identifier;
* every final ''secshare'';
* every final ''pubshare''.

A vector MUST provide enough information to verify

:''b<sub>0</sub> =
a<sub>0</sub> + tau mod q''

and, for ''j > 0'',

:''b<sub>j</sub> = a<sub>j</sub>''.

It MUST also permit verification that

:''B<sub>0</sub> =
A<sub>0</sub> + tau G''

and

:''B<sub>j</sub> = A<sub>j</sub>''

for ''j > 0''.

For every participant ''id'', the vector MUST permit verification of

:''secshare<sub>id</sub> =
PolynomialEvaluate(
final_coeffs,
Scalar(id + 1)
)''

and

:''pubshare<sub>id</sub> =
cbytes(secshare<sub>id</sub>G)''

and

:''pubshare<sub>id</sub> =
DerivePubshareFromCommitment(
id,
n,
t,
vss_commitment
)''.

Finally,

:''thresh_pk =
ThreshPubkeyFromCommitment(
t,
vss_commitment
)''

MUST hold.

=== Required Coverage ===

The test-vector set SHOULD include successful cases covering:

* ''1-of-1'';
* a nontrivial ''t-of-n'' configuration such as ''2-of-3'';
* ''t = n'';
* an imported threshold secret;
* a zero non-constant polynomial coefficient and its corresponding ''inf_point'' commitment; and
* successful reconstruction of the threshold public key from multiple valid signer subsets.

The test-vector set SHOULD also include failure cases for:

* zero and out-of-range imported secrets;
* malformed scalar encodings;
* malformed VSS commitment lengths;
* invalid extended-point encodings;
* an infinity constant-term commitment;
* a zero final participant secret share;
* an out-of-range participant identifier;
* a tampered participant secret share; and
* a tampered VSS commitment.

Failure vectors SHOULD identify the algorithm expected to fail and the class of failure, but implementations are not required to expose identical error messages.

=== RFC 9591 Cross-Check ===

Implementations SHOULD independently cross-check the underlying trusted-dealer Shamir and Feldman arithmetic against the FROST(secp256k1, SHA-256) material defined by RFC 9591 where a direct comparison is possible.

RFC 9591 participant evaluation points beginning at ''1'' correspond to this specification's zero-based participant identifiers according to

:''x = id + 1''.

The RFC comparison applies to the underlying pre-Taproot-safety sharing and commitment calculations.

The final outputs of this specification additionally include the mandatory Bitcoin-specific Taproot-safety transformation. Therefore final ''secshare'', ''pubshare'', ''vss_commitment'', and ''thresh_pk'' values are not required to equal otherwise corresponding RFC 9591 trusted-dealer values after that transformation has been applied.

== Reference Implementation ==

A reference implementation MAY accompany this specification to demonstrate the algorithms defined in [[#Algorithms|Algorithms]] and to generate and verify the test vectors defined in [[#Test Vectors|Test Vectors]].

Where a reference implementation is provided, it SHOULD implement the normative algorithms of this specification directly and SHOULD include tests covering the specified success and failure conditions.

The specification text is normative. If the behavior of a reference implementation conflicts with this document, implementations MUST follow this specification.

A reference implementation is intended for interoperability testing and review. Passing its test vectors does not by itself establish that an implementation provides production-grade randomness, side-channel resistance, secure secret storage, reliable memory erasure, or other operational security properties discussed in [[#Security Considerations|Security Considerations]].

== Out of Scope ==

This specification defines trusted-dealer generation and verification of long-term FROST key material for use with BIP 445.

The following are outside its scope:

* distributed key generation;
* share refresh, share repair, participant replacement, and proactive secret sharing;
* reconstruction or recovery of the complete threshold secret;
* FROST nonce generation, signing, signature-share verification, and signature aggregation;
* signing-session coordination or asynchronous signing protocols such as ROAST;
* concrete secret-share transport protocols and wire formats;
* wallet backup and recovery formats;
* hardware-security-module or secure-element interfaces;
* Taproot script-tree and control-block construction; and
* Bitcoin transaction construction, sighash computation, and consensus validation.

Where this specification imposes requirements on an out-of-scope mechanism, such as confidentiality and authentication of secret-share transport, those requirements remain normative even though the mechanism used to satisfy them is application-specific.

Other protocols MAY consume the key material produced by this specification provided that their key-format and security requirements are satisfied.

== Backwards Compatibility ==

This specification defines a new trusted-dealer key-generation procedure and does not replace or modify an existing Bitcoin consensus rule, network protocol, wallet format, or BIP 445 signing algorithm.

No backwards-compatibility mechanism is therefore required.

Key material produced according to this specification is intended to be directly usable with BIP 445 using the participant identifiers, secret-share encodings, public-share encodings, and threshold-public-key representation defined by this document.

Implementations using different key-generation conventions MAY require an explicit conversion or migration procedure and MUST NOT assume interoperability with key material produced by this specification.

